_010: Qwen2.5-1.5B LoRA K=4 random — KILLED during gen1 (CUDA OOM), only gen0 available\n\nKey context from the YAML:\n- The 0.6B \"resilience\" may actually be \"incapacity\" — the model is too small to drift. K=16 stress test (80% loss drop, still no diversity change) supports this alternative.\n- The 1.5B collapse cannot be causally separated from scale-inherent effects without the missing random baseline.\n- K × scale interaction is completely unexplored (only K=4 at 1.5B).\n- 0.6B and 1.5B use different model families (Qwen3 vs Qwen2.5), confounding scale and architecture.\n- The paper's original narrative was \"universal resilience\" but had to pivot to \"scale-conditional analysis\" after 1.5B results broke Claim 1.\n\n## Tables in the paper\n- Table 1: 8 configs at 0.6B, all P1=FAIL (no collapse). Vendi changes -0.28% to +1.13%.\n- Table 2: Vendi trajectory summary across scales. 0.6B stable, 1.5B collapsing.\n- Table 3: Format metrics. C1≈C5 on full-pool entropy drop (~-0.75 bits). K=8 amplifies to -1.13 bits.\n- Table 4: 1.5B trajectory over 5 gens. All diversity metrics decline monotonically, quality rises.\n- Table 5: Training loss dynamics. 0.6B K=16: 53% drop. 1.5B: 67% drop.\n\n## Your Review Task\nEvaluate the paper on:\n1. **Experimental design**: Are the experiments well-designed? Are controls adequate? Are there critical missing experiments?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Sample sizes, significance testing, reproducibility.\n3. **Claims vs evidence alignment**: Does each claim have sufficient supporting evidence? Are claims appropriately scoped?\n4. **Methodology**: Is the iterative training protocol sound? Are metrics appropriate?\n\nFor each issue, classify severity as CRITICAL / MAJOR / MINOR.\n\nOutput your review in this format:\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\nS1. ...\nS2. ...\n\n### Weaknesses\nW1. [SEVERITY] ...\nW2. [SEVERITY] ...\n\n### Questions for Authors\nQ1. ...\n\n### Missing Experiments\nM1. ...\n\n### Score: X/6\n### Confidence: X/5\n```\n","uuid":"09d9894d-7842-4400-83ae-810c773c61c8","session_id":"2b1f599e-fc53-49d6-adbf-f2a150d3d4f3"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **methods and experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"When Does Curation Become Dangerous? Scale-Dependent Diversity Collapse in Iterative Reward-Based Training\"\n\nThe paper studies diversity collapse in iterative self-consuming training with reward-based curation. It runs 8 configurations on Qwen3-0.6B (varying K∈{4,8,16}, LoRA vs full-FT, LR, curated vs random) over 3 generations, plus a cross-scale probe on Qwen2.5-1.5B over 5 generations.\n\nKey findings:\n1. At 0.6B, semantic diversity (Vendi Score) is stable across all 8 configs, even K=16 with 53% loss drop.\n2. Format homogenization (opening entropy, near-dup rate) is inherent to iterative self-training, not curation-caused (shown by C1 curated ≈ C5 random at K=4).\n3. LoRA creates a capacity artifact: curation appears to suppress diversity under LoRA but reverses to mild promotion under full-FT, with monotonic gradient across 3 capacity regimes.\n4. At 1.5B, Vendi Score declines 4.23% over 5 gens with monotonic nn-distance decline, rising self-BLEU, falling perplexity = multi-metric collapse.\n5. 1.5B random baseline was killed 3x by CUDA OOM, so causal attribution (curation-caused vs scale-inherent) is impossible.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe imagined experiment list includes 10 plans:\n- plan_001-008: 0.6B configs (LoRA K=4 curated, LoRA K=8 curated, LoRA K=4 random, Full-FT K=4 curated hi-LR, Full-FT K=4 curated lo-LR, Full-FT K=4 random, Full-FT K=16, C1 regen for format comparison)\n- plan_009: Qwen2.5-1.5B LoRA K=4 curated (5 gens) — completed, shows collapse (Vendi -4.23%)\n- plan_010: Qwen2.5-1.5B LoRA K=4 random — KILLED during gen1 (CUDA OOM), only gen0 available\n\nKey context from the YAML:\n- The 0.6B \"resilience\" may actually be \"incapacity\" — the model is too small to drift. K=16 stress test (80% loss drop, still no diversity change) supports this alternative.\n- The 1.5B collapse cannot be causally separated from scale-inherent effects without the missing random baseline.\n- K × scale interaction is completely unexplored (only K=4 at 1.5B).\n- 0.6B and 1.5B use different model families (Qwen3 vs Qwen2.5), confounding scale and architecture.\n- The paper's original narrative was \"universal resilience\" but had to pivot to \"scale-conditional analysis\" after 1.5B results broke Claim 1.\n\n## Tables in the paper\n- Table 1: 8 configs at 0.6B, all P1=FAIL (no collapse). Vendi changes -0.28% to +1.13%.\n- Table 2: Vendi trajectory summary across scales. 0.6B stable, 1.5B collapsing.\n- Table 3: Format metrics. C1≈C5 on full-pool entropy drop (~-0.75 bits). K=8 amplifies to -1.13 bits.\n- Table 4: 1.5B trajectory over 5 gens. All diversity metrics decline monotonically, quality rises.\n- Table 5: Training loss dynamics. 0.6B K=16: 53% drop. 1.5B: 67% drop.\n\n## Your Review Task\nEvaluate the paper on:\n1. **Experimental design**: Are the experiments well-designed? Are controls adequate? Are there critical missing experiments?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Sample sizes, significance testing, reproducibility.\n3. **Claims vs evidence alignment**: Does each claim have sufficient supporting evidence? Are claims appropriately scoped?\n4. **Methodology**: Is the iterative training protocol sound? Are metrics appropriate?\n\nFor each issue, classify severity as CRITICAL / MAJOR / MINOR.\n\nOutput your review in this format:\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\nS1. ...\nS2. ...\n\n### Weaknesses\nW1. [SEVERITY] ...\nW2. [SEVERITY] ...\n\n### Questions for Authors\nQ1. ...\n\n### Missing Experiments\nM1. ...\n\n### Score: X/6\n### Confidence: X/5\n```\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0171YWRYW2pbFasaRYcnpYd1","ses