{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AAnnJR4TNPneMGMEYiaiB5","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tIterative self-training with reward curation, where a reward model selects the best candidate response at each generation for supervised fine-tuning, is widely expected to erode output diversity through compounding selection pressure. We test this expectation with a controlled factorial experiment spanning 8 configurations, 3 iterative generations, and both Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) and full fine-tuning. The central finding contradicts the prevailing concern: semantic diversity, measured by Vendi Score over sentence embeddings, remains within $\\pm$1\\% of its initial value across all curated conditions, even at a 6.25\\% retention rate (top-1-of-16) where Vendi Score changes by only $-0.19\\%$. However, this stability masks what we term \\emph{metric-dependent collapse}. Iterative self-training causes substantial format homogenization regardless of curation strategy, with opening unique ratio falling from 0.757 to 0.477 and near-duplicate start rates rising from 37.9\\% to 75.6\\% under matched $K{=}4$ conditions, while embedding-based metrics remain blind to this convergence. We further document a direction reversal: curation suppresses diversity under LoRA ($-3.40$ percentage points relative to a random baseline) but promotes it under full fine-tuning ($+0.63$ pp), a pattern that tracks parameter capacity rather than selection intensity. These results establish that reward curation does not cause semantic collapse in iterative self-training, that diversity audits relying solely on embedding-based measures miss significant structural homogenization, and that conclusions about curation effects depend critically on the training regime under which they are measured."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0171YWRYW2pbFasaRYcnpYd1","session_id":"2b1f599e-fc53-49d6-adbf-f2a150d3d4f3","uuid":"2106c0c8-b5d8-4b1f-b1a2-e042fc4ab274","timestamp":"2026-05-17T14:42:53.927Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01DBw18kb8x1FFuiFuQsHEsK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Ep4rk8gSAitpEm7YG8Sziq","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/collapse_aware_reward_rebalancing/docs/paper/introduction.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2057,"cache_read_input_tokens":18034,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2057,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":73,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0171YWRYW2pbFasaRYcnpYd1","session_id":"2b1f599e-fc53-49d6-adbf-f2a150d3d4f3","uuid":"93f78195-0e6d-4210-a837-4a324e5a4c0f"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ep4rk8gSAitpEm7YG8Sziq","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t% Section 1: Introduction\n2\t% Note: \\section{} header is in main.tex\n3\t\n4\tIterative self-training with reward-based curation has become a prominent strategy for language model alignment. The current model generates multiple candidate responses per prompt, a reward model scores them, and the top-scoring response is selected for supervised fine-tuning to produce the next-generation model. This loop raises a fundamental stability question: if each generation retains only a narrow fraction of outputs (1 out of 4 or 1 out of 8 candidates), the compounding effect should progressively narrow the output distribution and erode the breadth that makes language models practical across diverse user needs.\n5\t\n6\tTwo research threads support this concern. The model collapse literature demonstrates that training on synthetic outputs leads to progressive distribution narrowing~\\cite{placeholder_shumailov, placeholder_alemohammad}: rare modes are underrepresented in synthetic samples, this compounds across iterations, and the model collapses onto high-density modes. Separately, RLHF studies document that single-round reward optimization reduces lexical and semantic variety~\\cite{placeholder_kirk}. Together, these establish a strong prior: iterative reward-curated self-training should cause diversity collapse with increasing severity at each generation.\n7\t\n8\tYet neither literature directly addresses the deployed setting. Model collapse results study unfiltered self-consumption where the model trains on its full output distribution; reward-curated pipelines apply an external selection filter, retain a fixed prompt scaffold across generations, and decode stochastically, all of which violate the theoretical preconditions for progressive narrowing. The RLHF literature examines single rounds rather than multi-generation iteration. No prior work has systematically measured whether diversity degrades across multiple generations of reward-curated fine-tuning under controlled factorial conditions.\n9\t\n10\tWe fill this gap with a controlled experiment spanning 8 configurations and 3 generations. The central result overturns expectations: semantic diversity (Vendi Score~\\cite{placeholder_vendi}) remains stable across all curated conditions ($-0.27\\%$ to $+0.91\\%$), including extreme selection pressure ($K{=}16$, 6.25\\% retention, $\\Delta = -0.19\\%$). However, this stability masks \\emph{metric-dependent collapse}: format diversity degrades substantially regardless of curation, with near-duplicate start rate rising from 37.9\\% to 75.6\\% and opening entropy dropping 0.75 bits at $K{=}4$. Standard embedding-based metrics are blind to this convergence. A second finding concerns the interaction between adaptation method and curation: under LoRA, curation suppresses Vendi Score by $-3.40$ pp relative to random, but under Full-FT with higher learning rate, curation promotes diversity by $+0.63$ pp. This pattern is consistent with a capacity artifact: LoRA's rank-16 bottleneck limits how much distributional structure the model can absorb, and the apparent suppression disappears when this constraint is removed.\n11\t\n12\tOur experimental design isolates four factors: adaptation method (LoRA versus Full-FT), selection pressure ($K \\in \\{4, 8, 16\\}$, corresponding to 25\\%, 12.5\\%, and 6.25\\% retention rates), learning rate ($5 \\times 10^{-6}$ versus $2 \\times 10^{-5}$), and curation strategy (reward top-1 versus random selection). All experiments use Qwen3-0.6B (596M parameters) as the base model, ArmoRM-Llama3-8B-v0.1~\\cite{placeholder_armorm} as the reward model, and a fixed set of 2{,}200 prompts from UltraFeedback. This factorial structure enables direct attribution of observed diversity changes to specific experimental factors rather than confounding variation in data composition or model scale.\n13\t\n14\tOur contributions are as follows:\n15\t\\begin{enumerate}\n16\t    \\item We provide systematic evidence that reward curation does not cause semantic diversity collapse in iterative self-training. Across 8 configurations and 3 generations, all curated conditions maintain Vendi Score within 1\\% of the initial value, even under extreme selection pressure ($K{=}16$, 6.25\\% retention). This result contradicts expectations from the model collapse literature.\n17\t    \\item We identify metric-dependent collapse: format diversity (response openings, structural templates) degrades substantially while embedding-based semantic diversity remains stable. This degradation occurs at comparable rates under curated and random selection, which indicates it is inherent to iterative self-training rather than caused by reward filtering. The effect intensifies with candidate pool size ($K{=}16$, $-$0.19\\% Vendi but 1.13 bits opening entropy loss at $K{=}8$). We propose two complementary indicators, \\texttt{opening\\_entropy} and \\texttt{near\\_dup\\_start\\_rate}, that capture this previously invisible dimension of homogenization, and recommend their inclusion in diversity audits alongside standard embedding-based measures.\n18\t    \\item We observe a direction reversal in the effect of curation across adaptation methods: curation suppresses diversity under LoRA ($-3.40$ pp) but promotes it under Full-FT ($+0.63$ pp), a pattern consistent with LoRA's rank-16 bottleneck constraining how much distributional structure the model can absorb from curated data.\n19\t\\end{enumerate}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0171YWRYW2pbFasaRYcnpYd1","session_id":"2b1f599e-fc53-49d6-adbf-f2a150d3d4f3","uuid":"fb9e591b-6217-4e8a-b2f6-de8f171ce3ad","timestamp":"2026-05-17T14:42:54.370Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GJxp8GRhr6tpF7X5J2n1YE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01ADFfrWAeELVe1Uy9r7KQJ6","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/collapse_aware_reward_rebalancing/docs/paper/experimental_setup.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2527,"cache_read_input_tokens":20091,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2527,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":65,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0171YWRYW2pbFasaRYcnpYd1","session_id":"2b1f599e-fc53-49d6-adbf-f2a150d3d4f3","uuid":"1cc5a432-650f-4e9d-9347-2a828d496298"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ADFfrWAeELVe1Uy9r7KQJ6","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t% Section 3: Experimental Setup\n2\t% Note: \\section{} header is in main.tex\n3\t\n4\t\\subsection{Iterative Self-Training Pipeline}\n5\t\\label{sec:pipeline}\n6\t\n7\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n8\t\\centering\n9\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig1_pipeline.pdf}\n10\t\\caption{Iterative self-training pipeline. Each generation generates $K$ candidates per prompt, scores them with ArmoRM, selects the top-1, and fine-tunes the base model for the next generation.}\n11\t\\label{fig:pipeline}\n12\t\\end{figure}\n13\t\n14\tWe study diversity dynamics through an iterative self-training loop (Figure~\\ref{fig:pipeline}). The base model is Qwen3-0.6B \\cite{placeholder_qwen3} (596M parameters), chosen as the smallest model capable of coherent instruction-following in tractable compute budgets. We use UltraFeedback \\cite{placeholder_ultrafeedback} as the prompt source, sampling 2{,}200 general instruction-following prompts (2{,}000 train, 200 eval). Each generation proceeds in four steps: (1) generate $K$ candidate responses per prompt; (2) score with ArmoRM-Llama3-8B-v0.1 \\cite{placeholder_armorm}; (3) select the top-1 candidate; (4) fine-tune the base model on the selected subset. We run three complete generations (Gen~0 $\\rightarrow$ Gen~1 $\\rightarrow$ Gen~2). Gen~0 metrics reflect the model after the first training round, not the unmodified base model. All candidate generation uses temperature $T{=}0.7$.\n15\t\n16\t\\subsection{Experimental Conditions}\n17\t\\label{sec:conditions}\n18\t\n19\tTable~\\ref{tab:conditions} summarizes the eight configurations. Our design isolates four factors through controlled comparisons. First, adaptation method (LoRA vs.\\ Full Fine-Tuning) isolates the effect of parameter capacity: LoRA \\cite{placeholder_lora} updates only 1.67\\% of parameters (rank 16), while Full-FT (Full Fine-Tuning) updates all parameters. Second, candidate pool size ($K \\in \\{4, 8, 16\\}$) isolates selection pressure, corresponding to 25\\%, 12.5\\%, and 6.25\\% retention rates respectively. Third, learning rate (5e-6 vs.\\ 2e-5 for Full-FT) isolates learning intensity across a conservative to aggressive optimization range. Fourth, curation strategy (ArmoRM top-1 vs.\\ random selection) isolates the causal effect of reward-based filtering: configurations C5, C6, and C7 serve as matched baselines for C1, C3, and C4 respectively and differ only in whether selection is reward-guided or random.\n20\t\n21\t\\begin{table}[t]\n22\t\\centering\n23\t\\small\n24\t\\caption{Experimental configurations. C1--C4 each has a matched random baseline (C5--C7); C8 extends the selection pressure range without a paired baseline. Trainable \\% refers to the fraction of model parameters updated during fine-tuning.}\n25\t\\label{tab:conditions}\n26\t\\resizebox{\\columnwidth}{!}{%\n27\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lllcll@{}}\n28\t\\toprule\n29\tID & Adaptation & $K$ & LR & Curation & Trainable \\\\\n30\t\\midrule\n31\tC1 & LoRA ($r{=}16$) & 4 & 2e-4 & ArmoRM top-1 & 1.67\\% \\\\\n32\tC2 & LoRA ($r{=}16$) & 8 & 2e-4 & ArmoRM top-1 & 1.67\\% \\\\\n33\tC3 & Full-FT & 4 & 5e-6 & ArmoRM top-1 & 100\\% \\\\\n34\tC4 & Full-FT & 4 & 2e-5 & ArmoRM top-1 & 100\\% \\\\\n35\tC8 & Full-FT & 16 & 2e-5 & ArmoRM top-1 & 100\\% \\\\\n36\t\\midrule\n37\tC5 & LoRA ($r{=}16$) & 4 & 2e-4 & Random 1-of-$K$ & 1.67\\% \\\\\n38\tC6 & Full-FT & 4 & 5e-6 & Random 1-of-$K$ & 100\\% \\\\\n39\tC7 & Full-FT & 4 & 2e-5 & Random 1-of-$K$ & 100\\% \\\\\n40\t\\bottomrule\n41\t\\end{tabular}}\n42\t\\end{table}\n43\t\n44\tThis factorial structure enables direct attribution. If reward curation causes diversity collapse, curated conditions (C1--C4, C8) should show steeper diversity decline than their random counterparts (C5--C7) across generations. If parameter capacity mediates collapse, LoRA and Full-FT conditions should diverge even under identical curation. If selection pressure drives collapse, C8 ($K{=}16$, 6.25\\% retention) should collapse faster than C2 ($K{=}8$) and C1 ($K{=}4$).\n45\t\n46\t\\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}\n47\t\\label{sec:metrics}\n48\t\n49\tWe evaluate diversity at two levels: semantic content and surface format.\n50\t\n51\t\\paragraph{Semantic diversity.} Our primary metric is the Vendi Score \\cite{placeholder_vendi}, a kernel-based diversity measure computed over sentence embeddings from all-MiniLM-L6-v2 \\cite{placeholder_minilm}. The Vendi Score equals the exponential of the Shannon entropy of the eigenvalues of the kernel matrix, with higher values indicating greater diversity. We complement this with Self-BLEU \\cite{placeholder_selfbleu}, defined as the average pairwise BLEU score among all responses in the evaluation set (lower indicates more diversity), and Nearest-Neighbor Distance (NND), the average cosine distance to the nearest neighbor in embedding space (higher indicates more diversity). All semantic metrics are computed over the 200 evaluation prompts with one response per prompt per generation.\n52\t\n53\t\\paragraph{Format diversity.} Standard embedding-based metrics can miss surface-level homogenization because semantically distinct responses may share identical templates. We introduce two format-level indicators. Opening Entropy measures the Shannon entropy of first-10-token sequences across responses; lower values indicate convergence toward fixed opening patterns. Near-Duplicate Start Rate measures the fraction of response pairs sharing a 50-character prefix; higher values indicate template repetition. These metrics capture a distinct failure mode: models that produce varied content but wrap it in repetitive formatting.\n54\t\n55\t\\paragraph{Quality and training dynamics.} We track mean ArmoRM score per generation to verify that quality does not degrade, and per-generation training loss to monitor learning dynamics and detect underfitting or overfitting.\n56\t\n57\t\\subsection{Training Details}\n58\t\\label{sec:training_details}\n59\t\n60\tBoth LoRA and Full-FT configurations use AdamW \\cite{placeholder_adamw} with cosine scheduling and train for 2 epochs per generation (3 for C4/C8). LoRA uses rank 16, $\\alpha{=}32$, applied to all linear layers. All experiments run on 2$\\times$ NVIDIA RTX 5090 GPUs. Complete hyperparameters are in Appendix Table~\\ref{tab:hyperparams}."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0171YWRYW2pbFasaRYcnpYd1","session_id":"2b1f599e-fc53-49d6-adbf-f2a150d3d4f3","uuid":"12993693-7829-4e40-9ed2-f71a8d84b51a","timestamp":"2026-05-17T14:42:57.221Z"}
