{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LBYfMWMjrEk6CYRd4JoEnH","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t% Section 4: Results\n2\t% Note: \\section{} header is in main.tex\n3\t\n4\t\\subsection{No Semantic Diversity Collapse}\n5\t\\label{sec:no_collapse}\n6\t\n7\tReward-based curation does not cause semantic diversity collapse over three generations of iterative self-training. Table~\\ref{tab:vendi_trajectory} reports Vendi Scores for all conditions across generations. Among the five curated configurations (C1--C4, C8), relative changes from Gen~0 to Gen~2 range from $-$0.27\\% to $+$0.91\\%, all within $\\pm$1\\%. This stability persists across every factor we vary: adaptation method (LoRA vs.\\ Full-FT), candidate pool size ($K \\in \\{4, 8, 16\\}$), and learning rate (5e-6 vs.\\ 2e-5). C8, with the strongest selection pressure ($K{=}16$, 6.25\\% retention), produces a change of only $-$0.19\\%. Both Full-FT curated conditions (C3, C4) exhibit monotonically increasing Vendi Scores, while LoRA conditions remain flat or fluctuate transiently before recovery.\n8\t\n9\t\\begin{table}[t]\n10\t\\centering\n11\t\\small\n12\t\\caption{Vendi Score across three self-training generations. $\\Delta$ denotes relative change from Gen~0 to Gen~2. All curated conditions remain within $\\pm$1\\%, while random baselines show larger positive drift.}\n13\t\\label{tab:vendi_trajectory}\n14\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}llcccc@{}}\n15\t\\toprule\n16\t& Config & Gen~0 & Gen~1 & Gen~2 & $\\Delta$ (\\%) \\\\\n17\t\\midrule\n18\t\\multicolumn{6}{@{}l}{\\textit{Curated (ArmoRM top-1)}} \\\\\n19\tC1 & LoRA, $K{=}4$ & 107.54 & 106.73 & 107.25 & $-$0.27 \\\\\n20\tC2 & LoRA, $K{=}8$ & 107.21 & 107.26 & 107.25 & $+$0.04 \\\\\n21\tC3 & Full-FT, 5e-6 & 107.10 & 107.61 & 108.07 & $+$0.91 \\\\\n22\tC4 & Full-FT, 2e-5 & 107.81 & 108.03 & 108.54 & $+$0.68 \\\\\n23\tC8 & Full-FT, $K{=}16$ & 107.32 & 106.73 & 107.11 & $-$0.19 \\\\\n24\t\\midrule\n25\t\\multicolumn{6}{@{}l}{\\textit{Random baseline (1-of-$K$)}} \\\\\n26\tC5 & LoRA, $K{=}4$ & 105.42 & 108.21 & 108.72 & $+$3.13 \\\\\n27\tC6 & Full-FT, 5e-6 & 107.17 & 107.87 & 108.64 & $+$1.37 \\\\\n28\tC7 & Full-FT, 2e-5 & 108.08 & 107.85 & 108.13 & $+$0.05 \\\\\n29\t\\bottomrule\n30\t\\end{tabular}\n31\t\\end{table}\n32\t\n33\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n34\t\\centering\n35\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_vendi_trajectories.pdf}\n36\t\\caption{Vendi Score trajectories across three self-training generations for all 8 configurations. Curated conditions (solid lines) remain within $\\pm$1\\% of Gen~0, while random baselines (dashed) show larger positive drift.}\n37\t\\label{fig:vendi_trajectories}\n38\t\\end{figure}\n39\t\n40\tFigure~\\ref{fig:vendi_trajectories} visualizes these trajectories. Secondary metrics corroborate this result. Self-BLEU stays below 0.01 for all curated conditions (absolute values 0.002--0.009), and Self-BLEU-4 computed over full training corpora confirms this pattern (all conditions $<$0.016; Appendix~\\ref{app:lexical_diversity}). Nearest-Neighbor Distance remains between 0.70 and 0.71 with no systematic contraction. These results contrast with predictions from the model collapse literature \\cite{placeholder_shumailov, placeholder_alemohammad}. The critical difference is that each generation draws fresh stochastic samples ($T{=}0.7$) from the current model, and the prompt set provides a constant external anchor.\n41\t\n42\t\\subsection{Relative Suppression, Not Absolute Collapse}\n43\t\\label{sec:suppression}\n44\t\n45\tCurated conditions do not collapse, but they grow diversity more slowly than matched random baselines. C5 and C6 show Vendi Score increases of $+$3.13\\% and $+$1.37\\% (Table~\\ref{tab:vendi_trajectory}), compared to $-$0.27\\% and $+$0.91\\% for their curated counterparts C1 and C3. This distinction matters: studies reporting that reward filtering reduces diversity relative to unfiltered baselines \\cite{placeholder_kirk} are sometimes read as evidence of degeneration. Our data show that a curated model holding steady has not collapsed, even if an uncurated model drifted above it. Evaluating curation requires comparing each model against its own starting point, not against a diverging uncurated trajectory.\n46\t\n47\t\\subsection{Direction Reversal Across Training Regimes}\n48\t\\label{sec:direction_reversal}\n49\t\n50\tThe curation gap (curated $\\Delta$ minus random $\\Delta$, in percentage points) reverses sign as training capacity increases. Under LoRA (C1 vs.\\ C5), the gap is $-$3.40~pp. Under Full-FT at 5e-6 (C3 vs.\\ C6), it contracts to $-$0.46~pp. Under Full-FT at 2e-5 (C4 vs.\\ C7), it reverses to $+$0.63~pp: curation now promotes diversity (Figure~\\ref{fig:direction_reversal}).\n51\t\n52\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n53\t\\centering\n54\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_direction_reversal.pdf}\n55\t\\caption{Curation gap (curated $\\Delta$ minus random $\\Delta$, in percentage points) across training regimes. The gap transitions from negative (curation suppresses diversity) under LoRA to positive (curation promotes diversity) under Full-FT with higher learning rate.}\n56\t\\label{fig:direction_reversal}\n57\t\\end{figure}\n58\t\n59\t\\begin{table}[t]\n60\t\\centering\n61\t\\small\n62\t\\caption{Training loss per generation. LoRA conditions converge to similar final loss regardless of curation strategy. C8 ($K{=}16$) reaches the lowest Gen~2 loss (0.097).}\n63\t\\label{tab:training_loss}\n64\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}llccc@{}}\n65\t\\toprule\n66\t& Config & Gen~0 & Gen~1 & Gen~2 \\\\\n67\t\\midrule\n68\tC1 & LoRA, curated & 0.413 & 0.240 & 0.166 \\\\\n69\tC2 & LoRA ($K{=}8$), curated & 0.411 & 0.239 & 0.168 \\\\\n70\tC5 & LoRA, random & 0.407 & 0.224 & 0.152 \\\\\n71\t\\midrule\n72\tC3 & Full-FT 5e-6, curated & 0.461 & 0.341 & 0.285 \\\\\n73\tC6 & Full-FT 5e-6, random & 0.461 & 0.335 & 0.277 \\\\\n74\t\\midrule\n75\tC4 & Full-FT 2e-5, curated & 0.380 & 0.212 & 0.140 \\\\\n76\tC8 & Full-FT 2e-5, $K{=}16$ & 0.206 & 0.129 & \\textbf{0.097} \\\\\n77\tC7 & Full-FT 2e-5, random & 0.377 & 0.199 & 0.132 \\\\\n78\t\\bottomrule\n79\t\\end{tabular}\n80\t\\end{table}\n81\t\n82\tTraining loss trajectories (Table~\\ref{tab:training_loss}) explain this reversal. Under LoRA, curated and random conditions converge to similar final loss (C1: 0.166, C5: 0.152). The rank-16 adapter's low-dimensional bottleneck limits how far any signal can reshape the output distribution. Random data, unconstrained by reward consistency, permits slightly more distributional drift within this bottleneck, producing the observed gap. Full-FT removes this constraint. At lr=2e-5, reward-selected data provides varied high-quality completions across topics that translate into greater semantic diversity when the model can fully absorb the curated distribution. The progression from LoRA through conservative Full-FT to aggressive Full-FT traces a continuum from parameter-limited to parameter-sufficient training.\n83\t\n84\tThis pattern is consistent with a capacity-mediated mechanism: LoRA's rank-16 bottleneck limits how much distributional structure the model can absorb, and the apparent suppression effect disappears when this constraint is removed. The practical implication is that experiments conducted exclusively with parameter-efficient methods may reach conclusions about curation's diversity impact that do not generalize to full fine-tuning.\n85\t\n86\t\\subsection{Quality-Diversity Non-Tradeoff}\n87\t\\label{sec:quality_diversity}\n88\t\n89\tCuration produces neither a quality gain large enough to motivate its use nor a diversity cost large enough to discourage it. Mean ArmoRM scores at Gen~2 range from 0.1096 (C5) to 0.1149 (C8), a spread of less than 5\\%. All conditions show mild quality decreases from Gen~0 to Gen~2 ($-$0.3\\% to $-$1.3\\%; full per-generation scores in Table~\\ref{tab:quality_scores}). The absence of meaningful quality improvement reflects saturation in the candidate quality distribution: the score gap between top-1 and average candidate is approximately 0.04, too narrow for the fine-tuned model to learn a meaningfully different quality level. The combined picture is that reward-based curation operates in a narrow effect regime at this scale: it does not collapse diversity, does not measurably improve quality, and its relative diversity effect depends more on training capacity than on curation strategy.\n90\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0171YWRYW2pbFasaRYcnpYd1","session_id":"2b1f599e-fc53-49d6-adbf-f2a150d3d4f3","uuid":"12a11e33-f181-44a2-9e57-2cb950d6b5c1","timestamp":"2026-05-17T14:42:57.680Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GJxp8GRhr6tpF7X5J2n1YE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015YRiLm8dbvGpfGH46A9E2w","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/collapse_aware_reward_rebalancing/docs/paper/analysis.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2527,"cache_read_input_tokens":20091,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2527,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":65,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0171YWRYW2pbFasaRYcnpYd1","session_id":"2b1f599e-fc53-49d6-adbf-f2a150d3d4f3","uuid":"f98af50f-6a08-4324-b2a9-bdec51172989"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015YRiLm8dbvGpfGH46A9E2w","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t% Section 5: Analysis\n2\t% Note: \\section{} header is in main.tex\n3\t\n4\t\\subsection{Metric-Dependent Collapse: Format vs.\\ Semantic}\n5\t\\label{sec:format_vs_semantic}\n6\t\n7\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n8\t\\centering\n9\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig4_format_collapse.pdf}\n10\t\\caption{Format diversity metrics across generations for LoRA $K{=}4$ curated (C1) and random baseline (C5). Both conditions show nearly identical format degradation, confirming that format collapse is driven by iterative self-training rather than reward curation.}\n11\t\\label{fig:format_collapse}\n12\t\\end{figure}\n13\t\n14\tDiversity collapse under iterative self-training is real but mischaracterized by standard embedding-based metrics. Semantic diversity as measured by Vendi Score remains stable across generations (Section~\\ref{sec:no_collapse}), yet surface-level formatting undergoes substantial homogenization. Table~\\ref{tab:format_collapse} presents format diversity metrics for the LoRA $K{=}4$ curated condition (C1) and the LoRA $K{=}4$ random baseline (C5) across Gen~0 and Gen~2. Opening unique ratio, defined as the fraction of responses with a unique first 50 characters, drops from 0.757 to 0.477 under curation and from 0.753 to 0.479 under the random baseline. Opening entropy (Shannon entropy over first-10-token sequences, in bits) declines by 0.75 bits under both conditions. The near-duplicate start rate (fraction of responses sharing a 50-character prefix with at least one other response) rises from 37.9\\% to 75.6\\% under curation and from 38.3\\% to 74.9\\% under the baseline. Both conditions exhibit nearly identical degradation trajectories (Figure~\\ref{fig:format_collapse}), which indicates that format collapse is an intrinsic property of iterative self-training rather than a consequence of reward curation. The C2 condition ($K{=}8$) provides further evidence: its opening entropy drops by 1.13 bits, compared to 0.75 bits for both $K{=}4$ conditions. Doubling $K$ amplifies homogenization, which suggests that candidate pool size acts as a scaling factor for format collapse. More candidates per prompt increase the probability of selecting responses that share common structural patterns and thereby reinforce template convergence across generations.\n15\t\n16\t\\begin{table}[t]\n17\t\\centering\n18\t\\small\n19\t\\caption{Format diversity metrics for LoRA $K{=}4$ curated (C1) and LoRA $K{=}4$ random baseline (C5). Both conditions show nearly identical degradation across all metrics, which confirms that format collapse is driven by iterative self-training rather than reward curation.}\n20\t\\label{tab:format_collapse}\n21\t\\resizebox{\\columnwidth}{!}{%\n22\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}\n23\t\\toprule\n24\tMetric & C1 Gen~0 & C1 Gen~2 & C5 Gen~0 & C5 Gen~2 \\\\\n25\t\\midrule\n26\tOpening unique ratio & 0.757 & 0.477 & 0.753 & 0.479 \\\\\n27\tOpening entropy (bits) & 12.43 & 11.68 & 12.43 & 11.68 \\\\\n28\tNear-dup start rate (\\%) & 37.9 & 75.6 & 38.3 & 74.9 \\\\\n29\tPer-response TTR & 0.682 & 0.670 & 0.683 & 0.660 \\\\\n30\tTrigram repetition rate & 0.174 & 0.256 & 0.174 & 0.258 \\\\\n31\t\\bottomrule\n32\t\\end{tabular}}\n33\t\\end{table}\n34\t\n35\tThe mechanism is self-reinforcing: when a model trains on its own outputs, frequent structural patterns are overrepresented in the training data, the fine-tuned model generates these patterns more often, and the cycle repeats. This loop operates on surface formatting while leaving semantic content untouched, regardless of whether training data is reward-curated or random. Per-response type-token ratio (TTR) drops by only 0.012 under curation compared to 0.023 under random, confirming that the effect is confined to structural formatting rather than vocabulary.\n36\t\n37\tThe Vendi Score's insensitivity has a technical explanation: the sentence embeddings (all-MiniLM-L6-v2) encode semantic content and are largely invariant to surface formatting. Two responses with identical openings but different topics receive distant embeddings. This disconnect means Vendi Score alone provides an incomplete picture of how iterative training reshapes the output distribution.\n38\t\n39\t\\subsection{Why Semantic Collapse Does Not Occur}\n40\t\\label{sec:why_no_collapse}\n41\t\n42\tWe hypothesize three contributing factors that together account for the observed semantic stability. First, \\textbf{prompt diversity} acts as an external anchor: 2{,}200 diverse prompts force broad semantic coverage at every generation regardless of per-prompt selection. Second, \\textbf{reward model saturation} limits selection pressure. The ArmoRM score gap between top-1 and pool average is approximately 0.04, so the curated candidate differs only marginally from a random alternative. Third, \\textbf{generation stochasticity} ($T{=}0.7$) injects fresh variation at each iteration, counteracting convergence to fixed outputs.\n43\t\n44\tThese factors connect to the sufficient conditions for model collapse identified by \\citet{placeholder_shumailov}: progressive distribution narrowing requires that synthetic data fully replaces external data and that sampling does not introduce compensating variance. In our setup, prompt diversity violates the first condition (an external scaffold persists across generations), and stochastic decoding violates the second. Reward saturation further weakens the selection channel through which curation could compound. C8 ($K{=}16$, 6.25\\% retention) provides the strongest test: under four times the selection pressure of $K{=}4$ configurations, Vendi Score changes by only $-$0.19\\% while training loss falls to 0.097. Even when curation retains fewer than 1 in 15 candidates, the semantic distribution remains stable. Isolating the individual contribution of each factor (e.g., through controlled ablation of prompt diversity or decoding temperature) remains an important direction for future work.\n45\t\n46\t\\subsection{LoRA as Capacity Bottleneck}\n47\t\\label{sec:lora_bottleneck}\n48\t\n49\tThe similarity of diversity outcomes between $K{=}4$ and $K{=}8$ LoRA conditions despite doubled selection pressure suggests that parameter capacity, not curation intensity, is the binding constraint under LoRA. The rank-16 adapter modifies only 1.67\\% of parameters, limiting how far any training signal can reshape the output distribution (see Appendix~\\ref{app:lora_bottleneck} for detailed analysis).\n50\t\n51\t\\subsection{Implications for Diversity Measurement}\n52\t\\label{sec:measurement_implications}\n53\t\n54\tStandard diversity evaluation relies on embedding-based metrics that are insensitive to format-level homogenization. Our results show this workflow misses an entire dimension of collapse visible to human readers.\n55\t\n56\tWe propose two complementary metrics: \\emph{opening entropy} (Shannon entropy over first-10-token sequences) and \\emph{near-duplicate start rate} (fraction sharing a 50-character prefix). Both require only string operations, need no GPU, and capture a failure mode orthogonal to semantic metrics. In our experiments, opening entropy drops 0.75 bits at $K{=}4$ (1.13 bits at $K{=}8$) while Vendi Score changes by only $-$0.27\\%, and near-duplicate start rate rises 38 pp while Self-BLEU remains below 0.007.\n57\t\n58\tThe broader lesson is that ``diversity'' in iterative self-training is not a single quantity. Format, semantic, and lexical diversity can evolve independently under the same procedure. A complete diversity audit should include at least one metric from each dimension."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0171YWRYW2pbFasaRYcnpYd1","session_id":"2b1f599e-fc53-49d6-adbf-f2a150d3d4f3","uuid":"27dd3b17-ba9b-454d-8938-f71a71e81b60","timestamp":"2026-05-17T14:42:58.025Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01MefMYo8uAQ6iZtDWYsUcaX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013w4D8h5fQdQZT973z4cAnC","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **novelty, positioning, and related work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"When Does Curation Become Dangerous? Scale-Dependent Diversity Collapse in Iterative Reward-Based Training\"\n\nThe paper studies diversity collapse in iterative self-consuming training with reward-based curation. It runs 8 configurations on Qwen3-0.6B (varying K∈{4,8,16}, LoRA vs full-FT, LR, curated vs random) over 3 generations, plus a cross-scale probe on Qwen2.5-1.5B over 5 generations.\n\nKey claims:\n1. Sub-1B resilience: reward-based curation does not cause semantic diversity collapse at 0.6B, even under extreme selection pressure.\n2. Scale-dependent phase transition: qualitative shift between 0.6B (stable) and 1.5B (collapsing) under identical protocols.\n3. Causal decomposition of format collapse: format homogenization is inherent to iterative self-training, not curation-caused (verified at 0.6B only).\n4. Capacity artifact: LoRA's low-rank constraint creates misleading diversity suppression, with direction reversal under full-FT across 3 capacity regimes.\n\n## Related Work Section (from paper)\nThe paper covers 4 areas:\n1. **Model collapse in self-consuming loops**: Shumailov et al. (2023), Alemohammad et al. (2023), Dohmatob et al. (2024). Paper claims to differ by studying reward-based curation specifically and finding scale-dependence.\n2. **Reward over-optimization**: Gao et al. (2023). Links 1.5B quality↑/diversity↓ pattern to reward over-optimization literature.\n3. **Diversity metrics**: Vendi Score (Friedman & Dieng 2023), Self-BLEU. Claims embedding metrics are blind to format homogenization.\n4. **LoRA and training capacity**: Hu et al. (2022). Claims capacity limitation creates measurement artifacts.\n\nTotal references: 8.\n\n## Key Context\n- The 1.5B random baseline was killed (CUDA OOM), so the \"phase transition\" claim cannot be causally verified.\n- 0.6B and 1.5B use different model families (Qwen3 vs Qwen2.5), confounding scale and architecture.\n- Only 2 scale points (0.6B, 1.5B) for the \"phase transition\" claim.\n- The paper pivoted from \"universal resilience\" narrative to \"scale-conditional analysis\" after 1.5B results broke the original framing.\n- All 0.6B experiments are on a single model family.\n\n## Your Review Task\nEvaluate the paper on:\n1. **Novelty**: Is the contribution sufficiently novel for a top venue? Is the \"phase transition\" framing justified by the evidence? Is the observation vs. mechanism distinction clear?\n2. **Related work coverage**: Are key related works cited? Is positioning against prior work accurate? Are there critical omissions?\n3. **Framing and claims**: Are claims appropriately stated? Is there overclaiming? Is the narrative coherent?\n4. **Significance and impact**: Would this paper change how practitioners or researchers think about iterative curation?\n\nFor each issue, classify severity as CRITICAL / MAJOR / MINOR.\n\nImportant related work areas to check coverage of:\n- Model collapse literature (Shumailov, Alemohammad, Dohmatob, Guo et al., Briesch et al., Hataya et al.)\n- RLHF/alignment diversity concerns (Kirk et al., Perez et al.)\n- Best-of-N sampling and rejection sampling literature\n- Data curation and quality filtering (e.g., FineWeb, DSIR)\n- Iterative DPO/self-play methods (SPIN, Self-Play Fine-Tuning)\n- Diversity-preserving training methods\n\nOutput your review in this format:\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\nS1. ...\nS2. ...\n\n### Weaknesses\nW1. [SEVERITY] ...\nW2. [SEVERITY] ...\n\n### Missing Related Work\nMR1. ...\n\n### Framing Issues\nF1. ...\n\n### Score: X/6\n### Confidence: X/5\n```\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":818,"cache_read_input_tokens":55814,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":818},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2b1f599e-fc53-49d6-adbf-f2a150d3d4f3","uuid":"07de1637-e351-445d-9ac8-df40cf0287e3"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a2b9968546496cfca","tool_use_id":"toolu_013w4D8h5fQdQZT973z4cAnC","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **novelty, positioning, and related work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"When Does Curation Become Dangerous? Scale-Dependent Diversity Collapse in Iterative Reward-Based Training\"\n\nThe paper studies diversity collapse in iterative self-consuming training with reward-based curation. It runs 8 configurations on Qwen3-0.6B (varying K∈{4,8,16}, LoRA vs full-FT, LR, curated vs random) over 3 generations, plus a cross-scale probe on Qwen2.5-1.5B over 5 generations.\n\nKey claims:\n1. Sub-1B resilience: reward-based curation does not cause semantic diversity collapse at 0.6B, even under extreme selection pressure.\n2. Scale-dependent phase transition: qualitative shift between 0.6B (stable) and 1.5B (collapsing) under identical protocols.\n3. Causal decomposition of format collapse: format homogenization is inherent to iterative self-training, not curation-caused (verified at 0.6B only).\n4. Capacity artifact: LoRA's low-rank constraint creates misleading diversity suppression, with direction reversal under full-FT across 3 capacity regimes.\n\n## Related Work Section (from paper)\nThe paper covers 4 areas:\n1. **Model collapse in self-consuming loops**: Shumailov et al. (2023), Alemohammad et al. (2023), Dohmatob et al. (2024). Paper claims to differ by studying reward-based curation specifically and finding scale-dependence.\n2. **Reward over-optimization**: Gao et al. (2023). Links 1.5B quality↑/diversity↓ pattern to reward over-optimization literature.\n3. **Diversity metrics**: Vendi Score (Friedman & Dieng 2023), Self-BLEU. Claims embedding metrics are blind to format homogenization.\n4. **LoRA and training capacity**: Hu et al. (2022). Claims capacity limitation creates measurement artifacts.\n\nTotal references: 8.\n\n## Key Context\n- The 1.5B random baseline was killed (CUDA OOM), so the \"phase transition\" claim cannot be causally verified.\n- 0.6B and 1.5B use different model families (Qwen3 vs Qwen2.5), confounding scale and architecture.\n- Only 2 scale points (0.6B, 1.5B) for the \"phase transition\" claim.\n- The paper pivoted from \"universal resilience\" narrative to \"scale-conditional analysis\" after 1.5B results broke the original framing.\n- All 0.6B experiments are on a single model family.\n\n## Your Review Task\nEvaluate the paper on:\n1. **Novelty**: Is the contribution sufficiently novel for a top venue? Is the \"phase transition\" framing justified by the evidence? Is the observation vs. mechanism distinction clear?\n2. **Related work coverage**: Are key related works cited? Is positioning against prior work accurate? Are there critical omissions?\n3. **Framing and claims**: Are claims appropriately stated? Is there overclaiming? Is the narrative coherent?\n4. **Significance and impact**: Would this paper change how practitioners or researchers think about iterative curation?\n\nFor each issue, classify severity as CRITICAL / MAJOR / MINOR.\n\nImportant related work areas to check coverage of:\n- Model collapse literature (Shumailov, Alemohammad, Dohmatob, Guo et al., Briesch et al., Hataya et al.)\n- RLHF/alignment diversity concerns (Kirk et al., Perez et al.)\n- Best-of-N sampling and rejection sampling literature\n- Data curation and quality filtering (e.g., FineWeb, DSIR)\n- Iterative DPO/self-play methods (SPIN, Self-Play Fine-Tuning)\n- Diversity-preserving training methods\n\nOutput your review in this format:\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\nS1. ...\nS2. ...\n\n### Weaknesses\nW1. [SEVERITY] ...\nW2. [SEVERITY] ...\n\n### Missing Related Work\nMR1. ...\n\n### Framing Issues\nF1. ...\n\n### Score: X/6\n### Confidence: X/5\n```\n","uuid":"b452a961-c50f-4f16-8e2e-ed7145b020cb","session_id":"2b1f599e-fc53-49d6-adbf-f2a150d3d4f3"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **novelty, positioning, and related work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"When Does Curation Become Dangerous? Scale-Dependent Diversity Collapse in Iterative Reward-Based Training\"\n\nThe paper studies diversity collapse in iterative self-consuming training with reward-based curation. It runs 8 configurations on Qwen3-0.6B (varying K∈{4,8,16}, LoRA vs full-FT, LR, curated vs random) over 3 generations, plus a cross-scale probe on Qwen2.5-1.5B over 5 generations.\n\nKey claims:\n1. Sub-1B resilience: reward-based curation does not cause semantic diversity collapse at 0.6B, even under extreme selection pressure.\n2. Scale-dependent phase transition: qualitative shift between 0.6B (stable) and 1.5B (collapsing) under identical protocols.\n3. Causal decomposition of format collapse: format homogenization is inherent to iterative self-training, not curation-caused (verified at 0.6B only).\n4. Capacity artifact: LoRA's low-rank constraint creates misleading diversity suppression, with direction reversal under full-FT across 3 capacity regimes.\n\n## Related Work Section (from paper)\nThe paper covers 4 areas:\n1. **Model collapse in self-consuming loops**: Shumailov et al. (2023), Alemohammad et al. (2023), Dohmatob et al. (2024). Paper claims to differ by studying reward-based curation specifically and finding scale-dependence.\n2. **Reward over-optimization**: Gao et al. (2023). Links 1.5B quality↑/diversity↓ pattern to reward over-optimization literature.\n3. **Diversity metrics**: Vendi Score (Friedman & Dieng 2023), Self-BLEU. Claims embedding metrics are blind to format homogenization.\n4. **LoRA and training capacity**: Hu et al. (2022). Claims capacity limitation creates measurement artifacts.\n\nTotal references: 8.\n\n## Key Context\n- The 1.5B random baseline was killed (CUDA OOM), so the \"phase transition\" claim cannot be causally verified.\n- 0.6B and 1.5B use different model families (Qwen3 vs Qwen2.5), confounding scale and architecture.\n- Only 2 scale points (0.6B, 1.5B) for the \"phase transition\" claim.\n- The paper pivoted from \"universal resilience\" narrative to \"scale-conditional analysis\" after 1.5B results broke the original framing.\n- All 0.6B experiments are on a single model family.\n\n## Your Review Task\nEvaluate the paper on:\n1. **Novelty**: Is the contribution sufficiently novel for a top venue? Is the \"phase transition\" framing justified by the evidence? Is the observation vs. mechanism distinction clear?\n2. **Related work coverage**: Are key related works cited? Is positioning against prior work accurate? Are there critical omissions?\n3. **Framing and claims**: Are claims appropriately stated? Is there overclaiming? Is the narrative coherent?\n4. **Significance and impact**: Would this paper change how practitioners or researchers think about iterative curation?\n\nFor each issue, classify severity as CRITICAL / MAJOR / MINOR.\n\nImportant related work areas to check coverage of:\n- Model collapse literature (Shumailov, Alemohammad, Dohmatob, Guo et al., Briesch et al., Hataya et al.)\n- RLHF/alignment diversity concerns (Kirk et al., Perez et al.)\n- Best-of-N sampling and rejection sampling literature\n- Data curation and quality filtering (e.g., FineWeb, DSIR)\n- Iterative DPO/self-play methods (SPIN, Self-Play Fine-Tuning)\n- Diversity-preserving training methods\n\nOutput your review in this format:\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\nS1. ...\nS2. ...\n\n### Weaknesses\nW1. [SEVERITY] ...\nW2. [SEVERITY] ...\n\n### Missing Related Work\nMR1. ...\n\n### Framing Issues\nF1. ...\n\n### Score: X/6\n### Confidence: X/5\n```\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_013w4D8h5fQdQZT973z4cAnC","session_id":"2b1f599e-fc53-49d6-adbf-f2a150d3d4f3","uuid":"ec020881-d42a-4fdd-8293-ffc2c3034268","timestamp":"2026-05-17T14:42:58.672Z"}
