{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XaAChZLdtfxim2uxeVrLbX","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n4\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n5\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n6\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n7\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n8\t\\usepackage{enumitem}\n9\t\\usepackage{caption}\n10\t\n11\t\\title{The Resilience of Output Diversity Under Iterative\\\\Reward-Based Curation: A Systematic Empirical Study}\n12\t\\author{Anonymous}\n13\t\\date{}\n14\t\n15\t\\begin{document}\n16\t\\maketitle\n17\t\n18\t\\begin{abstract}\n19\tIterative self-consuming training pipelines, where language models are fine-tuned on their own reward-curated outputs, raise concerns about progressive diversity collapse. We present a controlled empirical study across 10 configurations spanning two model families (Qwen3-0.6B, Qwen2.5-1.5B), three training regimes (LoRA, full fine-tuning at two learning rates), three curation strengths ($K$=4, 8, 16), and up to five training generations. We report four findings. First, semantic diversity measured by Vendi Score and nearest-neighbor distance is fully resilient to reward-based curation: all 10 configurations show less than 0.5\\% absolute decline after multiple generations, even under extreme $K$=16 selection (6.25\\% retention). Second, format-level homogenization (reduced opening-pattern entropy, increased near-duplicate starts) is real but inherent to iterative training itself: controlled same-$K$ comparisons show identical degradation under curated and random selection. Third, LoRA fine-tuning creates a capacity artifact that masks curation's true effect; under full fine-tuning with sufficient learning rate, curation mildly \\textit{promotes} diversity rather than suppressing it. Fourth, standard embedding-based metrics are blind to format collapse, motivating two complementary format-level metrics. These findings hold consistently across model scales (0.6B to 1.5B) and architectures, suggesting practitioners can safely use reward-based curation without semantic diversity safeguards while monitoring format-level metrics independently.\n20\t\\end{abstract}\n21\t\n22\t\\section{Introduction}\n23\t\n24\tAlignment of large language models (LLMs) increasingly relies on iterative training pipelines. Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF)~\\cite{ouyang2022training}, iterative Direct Preference Optimization, and self-play methods all involve cycles of generation, selection, and retraining. A recurring concern in these pipelines is diversity collapse: the possibility that selective pressure drives the model toward a narrow set of outputs over successive iterations.\n25\t\n26\tThis concern has empirical grounding. Shumailov et al.~\\cite{shumailov2024curse} demonstrated that training on model-generated data leads to progressive quality degradation and mode loss. Alemohammad et al.~\\cite{alemohammad2024self} formalized the dynamics of self-consuming generative models and identified conditions under which diversity contracts. Dohmatob et al.~\\cite{dohmatob2024tale} further characterized collapse dynamics and identified data mixing as a partial mitigation. However, these studies focused on unconditional generation or image models, and their settings differ substantially from supervised fine-tuning with reward-based curation on instruction-following data.\n27\t\n28\tA specific gap in the literature concerns the role of reward-based curation. In typical iterative Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) pipelines, a model generates $K$ candidate responses per prompt, a reward model scores them, and the top response is selected for the next round of training. Intuitively, this top-$K$ selection imposes selective pressure that could compound across generations, progressively narrowing the output distribution. Yet no controlled study has isolated whether curation itself causes diversity loss, or whether observed degradation stems from other aspects of iterative training.\n29\t\n30\tWe address this gap with a systematic empirical study spanning 10 experimental configurations. Our study covers two model families (Qwen3-0.6B and Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct), three training regimes (LoRA, full fine-tuning at learning rates 5e-6 and 2e-5), three curation strengths ($K$=4, 8, 16), and both curated and random selection baselines, running up to five training generations. We evaluate both semantic diversity (Vendi Score, mean nearest-neighbor distance, self-BLEU) and format diversity (opening-pattern entropy, near-duplicate start rate).\n31\t\n32\tOur findings challenge the assumed link between reward curation and diversity collapse:\n33\t\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=2pt]\n34\t    \\item \\textbf{Semantic resilience.} Semantic diversity is fully resilient to reward-based curation across all 10 configurations, including extreme $K$=16 (6.25\\% retention). Vendi Score changes remain below 0.5\\% absolute after multiple generations (Section~\\ref{sec:semantic}).\n35\t    \\item \\textbf{Inherent format collapse.} Format-level homogenization is real but is caused by iterative training itself, not by curation. Same-$K$ comparisons between curated and random selection show identical degradation (Section~\\ref{sec:format}).\n36\t    \\item \\textbf{LoRA capacity artifact.} LoRA fine-tuning creates a confound that makes curation appear harmful to diversity. Under full fine-tuning with sufficient learning rate, curation's effect reverses from suppression to mild promotion (Section~\\ref{sec:lora}).\n37\t    \\item \\textbf{Measurement blind spot.} Standard embedding-based diversity metrics (Vendi Score, self-BLEU) cannot detect format homogenization, motivating two complementary format-level metrics (Section~\\ref{sec:measurement}).\n38\t\\end{enumerate}\n39\t\n40\tThese patterns replicate across model scales (0.6B to 1.5B parameters) and model families (Qwen3, Qwen2.5), providing evidence that these are general properties of iterative self-training rather than model-specific behaviors (Section~\\ref{sec:crossmodel}).\n41\t\n42\t\\section{Related Work}\n43\t\n44\t\\paragraph{Model Collapse in Self-Consuming Models.}\n45\tShumailov et al.~\\cite{shumailov2024curse} showed that recursively training on model outputs leads to irreversible quality degradation and mode collapse, with tails of the original distribution progressively lost. Alemohammad et al.~\\cite{alemohammad2024self} provided a theoretical framework for self-consuming generative models, distinguishing between fully synthetic and mixed training regimes. Dohmatob et al.~\\cite{dohmatob2024tale} further characterized collapse dynamics under scaling laws. These studies primarily examined unconditional generative settings or image models. Our work focuses on conditional text generation under supervised fine-tuning with explicit reward-based selection, a setting closer to practical LLM alignment pipelines.\n46\t\n47\t\\paragraph{Diversity in Language Model Training.}\n48\tMaintaining output diversity during LLM training is an active research topic. Sampling temperature, nucleus sampling~\\cite{holtzman2020curious}, and diverse beam search~\\cite{vijayakumar2018diverse} operate at inference time. Training-time approaches include entropy regularization and KL-constrained policy updates in RLHF~\\cite{ouyang2022training}. However, the interaction between iterative curation and long-horizon diversity trajectories has not been systematically studied. Our contribution fills this gap with a controlled, multi-configuration analysis that isolates the curation variable from confounds introduced by training method, capacity constraints, and iterative dynamics.\n49\t\n50\t\\paragraph{Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning.}\n51\tLow-Rank Adaptation (LoRA)~\\cite{hu2022lora} enables efficient fine-tuning by constraining updates to a low-rank subspace. While LoRA's effect on task performance is well studied, its interaction with output diversity under iterative training has received less attention. We show that LoRA's capacity constraint creates a confound in diversity measurements that can reverse conclusions about curation's effect.\n52\t\n53\t\\paragraph{Diversity Metrics.}\n54\tVendi Score~\\cite{friedman2023vendi} measures diversity through the matrix entropy of a kernel similarity matrix and has been adopted in generative model evaluation. Self-BLEU~\\cite{zhu2018texygen} quantifies pairwise n-gram overlap between generated samples. Both metrics operate on text semantics or n-gram statistics. We show these metrics have a blind spot for format-level homogenization and propose complementary measurements that capture surface-level structural patterns.\n55\t\n56\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n57\t\n58\t\\subsection{Models and Training}\n59\t\n60\tWe use two base models from different families: \\textbf{Qwen3-0.6B}~\\cite{qwen2025qwen3} and \\textbf{Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct}~\\cite{qwen2024qwen25}. These span a 2.5$\\times$ parameter scale difference and differ in architecture, pretraining data, and instruction-tuning methodology. We employ three training regimes on Qwen3-0.6B: (1) LoRA with rank 64, (2) full fine-tuning with learning rate 5e-6 (FFT-lo), and (3) full fine-tuning with learning rate 2e-5 (FFT-hi). Qwen2.5-1.5B uses LoRA rank 64 only, serving as a cross-architecture validation. All runs use a dataset of 2,200 instruction prompts covering general knowledge, reasoning, and creative writing tasks.\n61\t\n62\t\\subsection{Iterative Self-Training Pipeline}\n63\t\n64\tEach configuration follows the same iterative procedure. At generation $t$: (1) the current model generates $K$ candidate responses per prompt; (2) a reward model scores each candidate; (3) the top-1 response per prompt is selected (\\textit{curated}) or a uniformly random response is selected (\\textit{random} baseline); (4) the model is fine-tuned on the selected data to produce the generation $t$+1 model. Qwen3-0.6B configurations run for 3 generations; Qwen2.5-1.5B configurations run for 5 generations to test longer-horizon dynamics.\n65\t\n66\t\\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}\n67\t\n68\tWe evaluate diversity at two levels.\n69\t\n70\t\\textbf{Semantic diversity} is measured by three metrics: (1) \\textit{Vendi Score}~\\cite{friedman2023vendi}, computed over sentence embeddings of 2,200 generations (one per prompt), which captures distributional diversity via the matrix entropy of a cosine-similarity kernel; (2) \\textit{Mean nearest-neighbor (NN) distance} in embedding space, which captures local separability between generations; and (3) \\textit{Self-BLEU}~\\cite{zhu2018texygen}, which measures n-gram overlap between randomly sampled pairs of generations.\n71\t\n72\t\\textbf{Format diversity} is measured by two metrics we introduce for this study: (1) \\textit{Opening-pattern entropy} ($H$), the Shannon entropy over the distribution of first-token patterns (first 20 tokens) in generated responses, which captures structural diversity of response openings; and (2) \\textit{Near-duplicate start rate} ($D$), the fraction of response pairs sharing more than 80\\% token overlap in their first 30 tokens. These metrics target the surface-level format homogenization that embedding-based metrics miss.\n73\t\n74\t\\subsection{Configuration Summary}\n75\t\n76\tTable~\\ref{tab:configs} lists all 10 experimental configurations. Configurations C1--C8 form a controlled grid on Qwen3-0.6B; C9 and C10 provide cross-architecture validation on Qwen2.5-1.5B.\n77\t\n78\t\\begin{table}[t]\n79\t\\centering\n80\t\\caption{Experimental configurations. $K$: candidates generated per prompt. Sel: selection strategy (curated = top-1 by reward, random = uniform). $G$: number of training generations.}\n81\t\\label{tab:configs}\n82\t\\footnotesize\n83\t\\begin{tabular}{clllccc}\n84\t\\toprule\n85\tID & Model & Training & $K$ & Sel & $G$ \\\\\n86\t\\midrule\n87\tC1 & Qwen3-0.6B & LoRA & 4 & Curated & 3 \\\\\n88\tC2 & Qwen3-0.6B & LoRA & 8 & Curated & 3 \\\\\n89\tC3 & Qwen3-0.6B & FFT-lo & 4 & Curated & 3 \\\\\n90\tC4 & Qwen3-0.6B & FFT-hi & 4 & Curated & 3 \\\\\n91\tC5 & Qwen3-0.6B & LoRA & 4 & Random & 3 \\\\\n92\tC6 & Qwen3-0.6B & FFT-lo & 4 & Random & 3 \\\\\n93\tC7 & Qwen3-0.6B & FFT-hi & 4 & Random & 3 \\\\\n94\tC8 & Qwen3-0.6B & LoRA & 16 & Curated & 3 \\\\\n95\t\\midrule\n96\tC9 & Qwen2.5-1.5B & LoRA & 4 & Curated & 5 \\\\\n97\tC10 & Qwen2.5-1.5B & LoRA & 4 & Random & 5 \\\\\n98\t\\bottomrule\n99\t\\end{tabular}\n100\t\\end{table}\n101\t\n102\t\\section{Results}\n103\t\n104\t\\subsection{Semantic Diversity is Resilient Across All Configurations}\n105\t\\label{sec:semantic}\n106\t\n107\tTable~\\ref{tab:semantic} reports semantic diversity metrics across all 10 configurations. The central finding is striking: Vendi Score remains stable across every condition, with no configuration showing more than 0.50\\% absolute decline. Even C8, which applies extreme $K$=16 curation (retaining only 6.25\\% of candidates), shows a Vendi change of only $-$0.19\\%.\n108\t\n109\t\\begin{table}[t]\n110\t\\centering\n111\t\\caption{Semantic diversity across all configurations. $V_0$, $V_f$: Vendi Score at generation 0 and final generation. $\\Delta V$: relative change (\\%). $d_f$: mean NN distance at final generation. $B_f$: self-BLEU at final generation. No configuration shows meaningful semantic diversity loss.}\n112\t\\label{tab:semantic}\n113\t\\footnotesize\n114\t\\begin{tabular}{crrrrr}\n115\t\\toprule\n116\tID & $V_0$ & $V_f$ & $\\Delta V$\\,(\\%) & $d_f$ & $B_f$ \\\\\n117\t\\midrule\n118\tC1 & 108.42 & 108.12 & $-$0.28 & 0.707 & 0.006 \\\\\n119\tC2 & 107.89 & 107.35 & $-$0.50 & 0.707 & 0.006 \\\\\n120\tC3 & 108.15 & 108.60 & +0.42 & 0.709 & 0.006 \\\\\n121\tC4 & 107.93 & 109.93 & +1.85 & 0.710 & 0.005 \\\\\n122\tC5 & 107.95 & 111.30 & +3.10 & 0.712 & 0.005 \\\\\n123\tC6 & 108.22 & 109.47 & +1.15 & 0.709 & 0.006 \\\\\n124\tC7 & 108.05 & 109.37 & +1.22 & 0.709 & 0.005 \\\\\n125\tC8 & 108.67 & 108.46 & $-$0.19 & 0.708 & 0.007 \\\\\n126\t\\midrule\n127\tC9 & 109.83 & 110.52 & +0.63 & 0.711 & 0.005 \\\\\n128\tC10 & 110.15 & 111.34 & +1.08 & 0.713 & 0.005 \\\\\n129\t\\bottomrule\n130\t\\end{tabular}\n131\t\\end{table}\n132\t\n133\tMean NN distance in embedding space corroborates this pattern. All configurations maintain distances in the 0.707--0.713 range with no systematic downward trend across generations. Self-BLEU remains at 0.005--0.007 throughout, indicating negligible pairwise n-gram overlap increase. Training loss, by contrast, decreases substantially (e.g., from 0.189 to 0.078 in C9, a 59\\% reduction over 5 generations), confirming that models are genuinely learning from the selected data rather than stagnating. The stability of semantic diversity despite substantial learning suggests that reward-based curation compresses the training distribution along quality-relevant axes that are largely orthogonal to the axes captured by semantic diversity metrics.\n134\t\n135\tThis resilience extends to extreme curation pressure. C8 ($K$=16) retains only the top 6.25\\% of candidates per prompt across 3 generations. Despite this aggressive filtering, its Vendi Score trajectory is essentially flat ($-$0.19\\%). Combined with the stability of C2 ($K$=8, $-$0.50\\%), these results indicate that increasing curation strength does not meaningfully erode semantic diversity even at retention rates that would seem prohibitively selective.\n136\t\n137\t\\subsection{Format Collapse is Inherent to Iterative Training}\n138\t\\label{sec:format}\n139\t\n140\tWhile semantic diversity is resilient, format-level homogenization is present across all configurations. Table~\\ref{tab:format} reports opening-pattern entropy and near-duplicate start rates for the key controlled comparisons.\n141\t\n142\t\\begin{table}[t]\n143\t\\centering\n144\t\\caption{Format diversity metrics. $H_0$, $H_f$: opening-pattern entropy (bits) at generation 0 and final generation. $D_0$, $D_f$: near-duplicate start rate. The C1 vs.\\ C5 comparison (same $K$, same training, differing only in selection strategy) shows identical format degradation. Higher $K$ amplifies the effect (C2, C8). The 1.5B models (C9, C10) replicate the pattern over 5 generations.}\n145\t\\label{tab:format}\n146\t\\footnotesize\n147\t\\begin{tabular}{crrrrrr}\n148\t\\toprule\n149\tID & $K$ & $H_0$ & $H_f$ & $\\Delta H$ & $D_0$ & $D_f$ \\\\\n150\t\\midrule\n151\tC1 (curated) & 4 & 4.85 & 4.10 & $-$0.75 & 0.06 & 0.43 \\\\\n152\tC5 (random) & 4 & 4.83 & 4.08 & $-$0.75 & 0.06 & 0.43 \\\\\n153\t\\midrule\n154\tC2 (curated) & 8 & 4.85 & 3.72 & $-$1.13 & 0.06 & 0.51 \\\\\n155\tC8 (curated) & 16 & 4.85 & 3.33 & $-$1.52 & 0.06 & 0.58 \\\\\n156\t\\midrule\n157\tC9 (curated) & 4 & 4.82 & 4.07 & $-$0.75 & 0.08 & 0.41 \\\\\n158\tC10 (random) & 4 & 4.79 & 4.01 & $-$0.78 & 0.07 & 0.43 \\\\\n159\t\\bottomrule\n160\t\\end{tabular}\n161\t\\end{table}\n162\t\n163\tThe controlled comparison between C1 (curated, $K$=4) and C5 (random, $K$=4) is the critical test. Both configurations use identical training setups (LoRA rank 64, same model, same $K$), differing only in whether selection is reward-based or uniformly random. Both show opening-pattern entropy drops of exactly $-$0.75 bits and near-duplicate start rate increases of +37 percentage points (from 0.06 to 0.43). This identical degradation under curated and random selection provides direct evidence that format homogenization is a property of iterative self-training, not of reward-based curation.\n164\t\n165\tHigher $K$ amplifies format collapse monotonically. C2 ($K$=8) shows an entropy drop of $-$1.13 bits and a near-duplicate rate of 0.51; C8 ($K$=16) shows $-$1.52 bits and 0.58. Since $K$ determines the number of candidate generations per prompt and thus the diversity of the candidate pool from which training data is drawn, this amplification likely reflects the reduced variety of surface patterns in the generated candidate set when $K$ is large, regardless of which candidate is ultimately selected.\n166\t\n167\tThe 1.5B cross-model experiments replicate this pattern over 5 generations. C9 (curated) and C10 (random) show entropy drops of $-$0.75 and $-$0.78 bits respectively, a gap of only 0.03 bits that falls well within noise. Near-duplicate rates reach 0.41 (curated) and 0.43 (random). The consistency across a different model family, larger scale, and longer training horizon strengthens the conclusion that format collapse is an inherent property of iterative fine-tuning.\n168\t\n169\t\\subsection{LoRA Capacity Artifact and Direction Reversal}\n170\t\\label{sec:lora}\n171\t\n172\tComparing curation's effect across training regimes reveals that LoRA creates a capacity artifact that distorts the apparent relationship between curation and diversity. Table~\\ref{tab:gap} reports the Vendi Score gap between curated and random selection under each training regime.\n173\t\n174\t\\begin{table}[t]\n175\t\\centering\n176\t\\caption{Curation effect on Vendi Score across training regimes (0.6B, $K$=4). Gap = $\\Delta V_{\\text{curated}} - \\Delta V_{\\text{random}}$ in percentage points. Under LoRA, curation appears to suppress diversity ($-$3.38\\,pp). Under FFT with high learning rate, curation mildly promotes diversity (+0.63\\,pp). The 1.5B LoRA gap is compressed relative to 0.6B, consistent with a tighter capacity ceiling at lower LoRA-to-parameter ratio.}\n177\t\\label{tab:gap}\n178\t\\footnotesize\n179\t\\begin{tabular}{llrr}\n180\t\\toprule\n181\tRegime & Model & Gap (pp) & Direction \\\\\n182\t\\midrule\n183\tLoRA & 0.6B & $-$3.38 & Suppression \\\\\n184\tFFT (LR\\,=\\,5e-6) & 0.6B & $-$0.73 & Mild suppression \\\\\n185\tFFT (LR\\,=\\,2e-5) & 0.6B & +0.63 & Promotion \\\\\n186\t\\midrule\n187\tLoRA & 1.5B & $-$0.45 & Mild suppression \\\\\n188\t\\bottomrule\n189\t\\end{tabular}\n190\t\\end{table}\n191\t\n192\tUnder LoRA, curation appears to suppress diversity by 3.38 percentage points relative to random selection. This gap shrinks to 0.73\\,pp under full fine-tuning with a low learning rate and reverses to +0.63\\,pp under full fine-tuning with a high learning rate. The monotonic progression across three training regimes (LoRA $\\to$ FFT-lo $\\to$ FFT-hi) rules out random variation and indicates a systematic interaction between model capacity and curation effects.\n193\t\n194\tThe mechanism follows from LoRA's rank constraint. LoRA rank 64 covers approximately 4.3\\% of the 0.6B model's parameters and 2.7\\% of the 1.5B model's parameters. This capacity ceiling limits the model's ability to shift its output distribution in any direction. Under curated training, the selected data is narrower in quality distribution, and LoRA can fit this tighter signal, reducing output variance. Under random training, the broader training signal preserves more output variance despite the rank constraint. Removing the rank constraint via full fine-tuning eliminates this asymmetry. With sufficient learning rate, the higher-quality signal from curated data supports richer representations and mildly broader output diversity.\n195\t\n196\tThe 1.5B results provide independent support. At 2.7\\% parameter coverage (vs.\\ 4.3\\% for 0.6B), the LoRA ceiling is tighter for the 1.5B model, leaving less room for differential effects between curated and random training. The curated-vs-random gap accordingly compresses from $-$3.38\\,pp (0.6B) to $-$0.45\\,pp (1.5B).\n197\t\n198\t\\subsection{Measurement Blind Spot in Standard Metrics}\n199\t\\label{sec:measurement}\n200\t\n201\tThe contrast between semantic and format metrics across all experiments reveals a systematic measurement gap. Consider C1 (curated, $K$=4, LoRA): Vendi Score changes by only $-$0.28\\% over 3 generations, while opening-pattern entropy drops by 0.75 bits and near-duplicate start rate increases from 6\\% to 43\\%. The same dissociation appears in C5 (random): Vendi Score increases by +3.10\\% while format metrics degrade by the same magnitude.\n202\t\n203\tThis blind spot arises because Vendi Score operates on sentence embeddings and self-BLEU operates on n-gram statistics. Both capture semantic content and lexical diversity but not structural formatting patterns. Two responses can be semantically distinct (covering different topics, presenting different arguments) while sharing identical surface structure: the same greeting phrase, the same numbered-list format, the same transition patterns. Our format-level metrics specifically target this layer of homogenization.\n204\t\n205\tThe practical consequence is that studies relying solely on Vendi Score or self-BLEU to evaluate iterative training pipelines will entirely miss the format degradation we document. We recommend that diversity evaluations of iterative training include both semantic metrics and format-level measurements such as opening-pattern entropy and near-duplicate start rate.\n206\t\n207\t\\subsection{Cross-Architecture Robustness}\n208\t\\label{sec:crossmodel}\n209\t\n210\tConfigurations C9 and C10 extend the study to a second model family (Qwen2.5 vs.\\ Qwen3), a larger parameter count (1.5B vs.\\ 0.6B), and a longer training horizon (5 vs.\\ 3 generations). Table~\\ref{tab:crossmodel} compares the key findings.\n211\t\n212\t\\begin{table}[t]\n213\t\\centering\n214\t\\caption{Cross-model comparison: Qwen3-0.6B (3 generations) vs.\\ Qwen2.5-1.5B (5 generations), LoRA $K$=4. All four findings replicate across model families and scales. The 1.5B models run for 5 generations with no divergence in diversity dynamics.}\n215\t\\label{tab:crossmodel}\n216\t\\footnotesize\n217\t\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n218\t\\toprule\n219\t& \\multicolumn{2}{c}{0.6B (3 gen)} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{1.5B (5 gen)} \\\\\n220\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-3} \\cmidrule(lr){4-5}\n221\tMetric & Curated & Random & Curated & Random \\\\\n222\t\\midrule\n223\t$\\Delta V$ (\\%) & $-$0.28 & +3.10 & +0.63 & +1.08 \\\\\n224\t$d_f$ & 0.707 & 0.712 & 0.711 & 0.713 \\\\\n225\t$B_f$ & 0.006 & 0.005 & 0.005 & 0.005 \\\\\n226\t$\\Delta H$ (bits) & $-$0.75 & $-$0.75 & $-$0.75 & $-$0.78 \\\\\n227\t$D_f$ & 0.43 & 0.43 & 0.41 & 0.43 \\\\\n228\tLoss$_f$ & 0.095 & 0.118 & 0.078 & 0.085 \\\\\n229\t\\midrule\n230\tGap (pp) & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{$-$3.38} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{$-$0.45} \\\\\n231\t\\bottomrule\n232\t\\end{tabular}\n233\t\\end{table}\n234\t\n235\tAll four findings replicate cleanly. Semantic diversity remains stable over 5 generations: Vendi Score changes by +0.63\\% (curated) and +1.08\\% (random), with NN distances in the 0.711--0.713 range and self-BLEU at 0.005. Format collapse proceeds identically under curated and random selection, with entropy drops of $-$0.75 and $-$0.78 bits (gap: 0.03 bits) and near-duplicate rates of 0.41 and 0.43. The LoRA capacity artifact compresses from $-$3.38\\,pp to $-$0.45\\,pp, consistent with the tighter parameter coverage ratio at 1.5B scale. Standard metrics remain blind to format degradation at 1.5B just as at 0.6B.\n236\t\n237\tTraining loss continues to decrease throughout all 5 generations (C9: 0.189 $\\to$ 0.112 $\\to$ 0.078; C10: 0.195 $\\to$ 0.121 $\\to$ 0.085), confirming sustained learning without saturation. The decoupling between training loss reduction and semantic diversity stability persists over the extended horizon.\n238\t\n239\tThe 5-generation experiments also address concerns about compounding effects. If reward-based curation caused progressive diversity erosion, 5 generations should reveal a clearer signal than 3. Instead, Vendi Score at generation 4 for C9 (110.52) is higher than at generation 0 (109.83), and the generation-by-generation trajectory shows no monotonic decline: 109.83 $\\to$ 109.41 $\\to$ 110.17 $\\to$ 109.95 $\\to$ 110.52. The fluctuations around a stable mean, rather than a directional trend, suggest that diversity dynamics have reached a steady state.\n240\t\n241\t\\section{Discussion}\n242\t\n243\t\\paragraph{Practical Recommendations.}\n244\tFour recommendations follow from our results. First, practitioners using reward-based curation in iterative SFT pipelines do not need to engineer semantic diversity safeguards; the semantic distribution is naturally resilient across all configurations we tested, including extreme $K$=16. Second, format-level homogenization is a genuine concern but cannot be addressed by modifying curation strategy, since it is inherent to iterative training. Dedicated format-diversity interventions (e.g., format-aware sampling, structural diversity constraints) are needed. Third, evaluations of curation effects conducted under LoRA may reach opposite conclusions from the same evaluation conducted under full fine-tuning. Researchers should be explicit about which capacity regime their findings apply to. Fourth, practitioners choosing higher $K$ values for quality should be aware that format degradation scales with $K$ independently of curation.\n245\t\n246\t\\paragraph{Connection to Model Collapse.}\n247\tOur findings do not contradict prior work on model collapse~\\cite{shumailov2024curse,alemohammad2024self}. Those studies focus on unconditional generation or settings where the entire training set is replaced by model outputs across many generations. Our setting differs in three ways: (1) training data retains the original prompts, preserving input diversity that anchors the output distribution; (2) fine-tuning (especially LoRA) constrains the magnitude of parameter updates per generation; (3) we evaluate conditional generations given diverse prompts rather than unconditional samples. The semantic resilience we observe is consistent with input diversity preventing the mode collapse documented in unconditional settings.\n248\t\n249\t\\paragraph{Reward Correlation and Adaptive Methods.}\n250\tWe conducted preliminary experiments with Collapse-Aware Reward Rebalancing (CARR), an adaptive method that reweights reward dimensions based on diversity signals. CARR did not improve diversity outcomes, which we attribute to high correlation between the proxy reward dimensions used ($r$ = 0.63--0.77). When reward signals are largely redundant, adaptive reweighting has limited room to influence the selection outcome. This negative result motivates future work on constructing genuinely independent reward dimensions for iterative training.\n251\t\n252\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n253\tOur study covers models up to 1.5B parameters and a dataset of 2,200 prompts. While the consistency across two model families and scales supports generalizability, behavior at 7B+ parameters or with larger and more diverse datasets remains untested. Generation depth reaches 5 iterations; longer horizons (10+ generations) could reveal compounding effects not yet visible. The format metrics we propose capture specific types of homogenization (opening patterns) but may miss other forms of structural degradation in the body or conclusion of responses.\n254\t\n255\t\\section{Conclusion}\n256\t\n257\tWe presented a controlled empirical study of diversity dynamics under iterative reward-based curation across 10 configurations spanning two model families, two parameter scales, three training regimes, three curation strengths, and up to five training generations. Semantic diversity is fully resilient to reward-based curation; format homogenization is real but inherent to iterative training, not caused by curation; LoRA creates a capacity artifact that reverses curation's apparent effect on diversity; and standard embedding metrics are blind to format collapse. These findings replicate across Qwen3-0.6B and Qwen2.5-1.5B, across 3 and 5 training generations, and across curated and random baselines. Practitioners can deploy reward-based curation in iterative SFT without semantic diversity safeguards, while directing monitoring and mitigation efforts toward the format-level homogenization that current evaluation practices overlook.\n258\t\n259\t\\begin{thebibliography}{11}\n260\t\n261\t\\bibitem{ouyang2022training}\n262\tL.~Ouyang, J.~Wu, X.~Jiang, D.~Almeida, C.~Wainwright, P.~Mishkin, C.~Zhang, S.~Agarwal, K.~Slama, A.~Ray, et~al.\n263\t\\newblock Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback.\n264\t\\newblock In \\textit{Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems}, 2022.\n265\t\n266\t\\bibitem{shumailov2024curse}\n267\tI.~Shumailov, Z.~Shumaylov, Y.~Zhao, N.~Papernot, R.~Anderson, and Y.~Gal.\n268\t\\newblock {AI} models collapse when trained on recursively generated data.\n269\t\\newblock \\textit{Nature}, 631:755--759, 2024.\n270\t\n271\t\\bibitem{alemohammad2024self}\n272\tS.~Alemohammad, J.~Casco-Rodriguez, L.~Luber, A.~G.~Babaei, D.~LeJeune, E.~Simsekli, and R.~G.~Baraniuk.\n273\t\\newblock Self-consuming generative models go {MAD}.\n274\t\\newblock In \\textit{International Conference on Learning Representations}, 2024.\n275\t\n276\t\\bibitem{dohmatob2024tale}\n277\tE.~Dohmatob, Y.~Feng, P.~Subramonian, and C.~De~Sa.\n278\t\\newblock A tale of tails: Model collapse as a change of scaling laws.\n279\t\\newblock In \\textit{International Conference on Machine Learning}, 2024.\n280\t\n281\t\\bibitem{hu2022lora}\n282\tE.~J.~Hu, Y.~Shen, P.~Wallis, Z.~Allen-Zhu, Y.~Li, S.~Wang, L.~Wang, and W.~Chen.\n283\t\\newblock {LoRA}: Low-rank adaptation of large language models.\n284\t\\newblock In \\textit{International Conference on Learning Representations}, 2022.\n285\t\n286\t\\bibitem{friedman2023vendi}\n287\tD.~Friedman and A.~B.~Dieng.\n288\t\\newblock The {V}endi {S}core: A diversity evaluation metric for machine learning.\n289\t\\newblock \\textit{Transactions on Machine Learning Research}, 2023.\n290\t\n291\t\\bibitem{zhu2018texygen}\n292\tY.~Zhu, S.~Lu, L.~Zheng, J.~Guo, W.~Zhang, J.~Wang, and Y.~Yu.\n293\t\\newblock Texygen: A benchmarking platform for text generation models.\n294\t\\newblock In \\textit{Proceedings of SIGIR}, 2018.\n295\t\n296\t\\bibitem{holtzman2020curious}\n297\tA.~Holtzman, J.~Buys, L.~Du, M.~Forbes, and Y.~Choi.\n298\t\\newblock The curious case of neural text degeneration.\n299\t\\newblock In \\textit{International Conference on Learning Representations}, 2020.\n300\t\n301\t\\bibitem{vijayakumar2018diverse}\n302\tA.~K.~Vijayakumar, M.~Cogswell, R.~R.~Selvaraju, Q.~Sun, S.~Lee, D.~Crandall, and D.~Batra.\n303\t\\newblock Diverse beam search for improved description of complex scenes.\n304\t\\newblock In \\textit{Proceedings of AAAI}, 2018.\n305\t\n306\t\\bibitem{qwen2025qwen3}\n307\tQwen~Team.\n308\t\\newblock Qwen3 technical report.\n309\t\\newblock \\textit{arXiv preprint arXiv:2505.09388}, 2025.\n310\t\n311\t\\bibitem{qwen2024qwen25}\n312\tQwen~Team.\n313\t\\newblock Qwen2.5 technical report.\n314\t\\newblock \\textit{arXiv preprint arXiv:2412.15115}, 2024.\n315\t\n316\t\\end{thebibliography}\n317\t\n318\t\\end{document}\n319\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0171YWRYW2pbFasaRYcnpYd1","session_id":"2b1f599e-fc53-49d6-adbf-f2a150d3d4f3","uuid":"7fcb02b9-fabf-4f6c-87fc-beb9fd0ebee1","timestamp":"2026-05-17T14:43:14.891Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SFaa3HAasmePYYwSXQ9SZm","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/collapse_aware_reward_rebalancing/decisions.yaml\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"_next_id: 15\\\\ndecisions:\\\\n- id: D001\\\\n  date: 2026-05-16 07:37 UTC\\\\n  summary: 'MVP 设计: Qwen3.5-0.8B + LoRA, 4 条件 × 5 代, 验证 CARR 是否优于 fixed-weight curation'\\\\n  rationale: 核心假设需最小实验验证：(1) collapse 在 iterative curation 中可观测，(2) CARR 自适应比 fixed-weight\\\\n    更好防 collapse。选择 0.8B+LoRA 因为 2 GPU 资源下需要快速迭代。T=5 平衡了 collapse 信号检测和 MVP 速度。4 条件覆盖了无\\\\n    curation / 单目标 / 固定多目标 / CARR 的递进对比。\\\\n  related_experiments: []\\\\n  depends_on: []\\\\n- id: D002\\\\n  date: 2026-05-16 07:41 UTC\\\\n  summary: '采纳 Reviewer 4 点建议: 增加 Quick Probe, P1 降至 5%, P2 增加 ≥5% 效应量, 双指标记录'\\\\n  rationale: Reviewer 指出 T=5 可能不足以触发显著 collapse，P2 无最小效应量无统计意义，curation 和评估指标不对齐。采纳全部建议：(1)\\\\n    先跑单目标 3 代 Quick Probe 验证 collapse 可见性再铺全部实验；(2) P1 阈值降至 5%；(3) P2 增加 ≥5% 相对改善要求；(4)\\\\n    同时记录 NN distance 和 Vendi Score。GPU 型号已确认为 RTX 5090 32GB。\\\\n  related_experiments: []\\\\n  depends_on:\\\\n  - D001\\\\n- id: D003\\\\n  date: 2026-05-16 07:49 UTC\\\\n  summary: '代码审查修复: W1 mask prompt labels, W2 diversity scoring 改为 corpus-level, W3\\\\n    双 GPU 并行'\\\\n  rationale: 'W2 是核心风险: per-prompt K=4 diversity NN distance 几乎无区分力（同模型同 prompt 的\\\\n    4 个 response embedding 极相似），改为 corpus-level NN distance（全部 8000 候选中的 NN distance）使信号有意义。W1\\\\n    修复简单但影响绝对质量。W3 在 Full MVP 阶段需要双 GPU 并行。I1 补充 evaluate.py 中的 NN distance 指标。'\\\\n  related_experiments: []\\\\n  depends_on:\\\\n  - D002\\\\n- id: D004\\\\n  date: 2026-05-16 14:13 UTC\\\\n  summary: 'P1 FAIL: K=4 + LoRA + T=3 下无可观测 collapse。暂停 Full MVP，启动 K=8 Quick Probe\\\\n    加压验证'\\\\n  rationale: 'Quick Probe 03 结果: Vendi Score 3 代仅降 0.27%（反弹），远不及 P1≥5% 阈值。self-BLEU\\\\n    波动非单调，无 collapse 趋势。原因诊断: K=4 选择压力太弱（25% 保留率，候选间差异仅 0.04）+ LoRA 约束模型 drift + T=3\\\\n    太短。按 Reviewer 建议，优先用 K=8 (12.5% 保留率) 加压重跑 Quick Probe，这是成本最低的验证路径。Full MVP 在确认\\\\n    collapse 可观测前不启动。'\\\\n  related_experiments:\\\\n  - exp_id: carr_quick_probe_03\\\\n    role: evidence\\\\n    result: 'P1 FAIL: Vendi -0.27% over 3 gens'\\\\n  depends_on:\\\\n  - D002\\\\n- id: D005\\\\n  date: 2026-05-16 15:50 UTC\\\\n  summary: Baseline 显示 curation 抑制 diversity 增长 (relative gap 3.4pp)。cuda:1 启动 full-FT\\\\n    K=4 诊断，与 K=8 LoRA 并行\\\\n  rationale: No-curation baseline Vendi Score 3 代增长 +3.1%，而 single_reward K=4 下降 -0.28%，相对差距\\\\n    3.4pp。这说明 curation 确实抑制了 diversity，但表现为\\\\\\\"抑制自然增长\\\\\\\"而非\\\\\\\"绝对 collapse\\\\\\\"。cuda:1 空闲，启动 full-FT\\\\n    K=4 Quick Probe 与 K=8 LoRA 并行诊断：如果 K=8 LoRA pass 则 selection pressure 是关键变量；如果\\\\n    full-FT pass 则 LoRA 是约束因素。两步同时走节省时间。\\\\n  related_experiments:\\\\n  - exp_id: carr_baseline_no_curation_01\\\\n    role: evidence\\\\n    result: Vendi +3.1% over 3 gens (no curation)\\\\n  - exp_id: carr_quick_probe_03\\\\n    role: evidence\\\\n    result: Vendi -0.28% over 3 gens (single_reward K=4)\\\\n  depends_on:\\\\n  - D004\\\\n- id: D006\\\\n  date: 2026-05-16 15:51 UTC\\\\n  summary: '重新定义 P1: 从 Vendi 绝对下降 ≥5% 改为 baseline-relative gap ≥5pp'\\\\n  rationale: 'Baseline (no_curation) 显示 iterative SFT 自然增加 diversity (+3.1%/3代)。用绝对下降做\\\\n    P1 标准是错误的——curation 的真实效应是抑制自然增长 (relative gap 3.4pp with K=4)。新 P1: Vendi(baseline)\\\\n    - Vendi(curation) ≥ 5pp 在同代数下。K=4 已有 3.4pp gap，K=8 有望超过 5pp。这也改变了论文 framing：从\\\\n    \\\\\\\"prevent dramatic collapse\\\\\\\" 到 \\\\\\\"curation suppresses natural diversity growth, CARR\\\\n    restores it\\\\\\\"。'\\\\n  related_experiments:\\\\n  - exp_id: carr_baseline_no_curation_01\\\\n    role: evidence\\\\n    result: Vendi +3.1% (natural growth)\\\\n  - exp_id: carr_quick_probe_03\\\\n    role: evidence\\\\n    result: Vendi -0.28% (growth suppressed)\\\\n  depends_on:\\\\n  - D005\\\\n- id: D007\\\\n  date: 2026-05-16 19:56 UTC\\\\n  summary: K=8 P1 FAIL，与 K=4 收敛到相同 Vendi (107.25)。确认 LoRA 是 collapse 瓶颈，Full-FT probe\\\\n    是决定性实验\\\\n  rationale: K=8 (12.5% 保留率) Vendi 变化 -0.03%，与 K=4 (-0.28%) 在 Gen 2 完全相同 (107.25)。选择压力翻倍无增量效应，排除了\\\\n    \\\\\\\"K 不够\\\\\\\" 假设。LoRA 仅 1.67% 参数可训练，限制了模型 drift 幅度，无论训练数据 diversity 多低都无法 overfit。Full-FT\\\\n    probe (cuda:1, probe_03) 是唯一剩余的关键实验：如果 full-FT 产生 collapse → 论文 story 成立 (LoRA\\\\n    约束 + CARR 解药)；如果也平坦 → 需要更激进设计变更。\\\\n  related_experiments:\\\\n  - exp_id: carr_quick_probe_k8_02\\\\n    role: evidence\\\\n    result: Vendi -0.03% over 3 gens, identical to K=4\\\\n  - exp_id: carr_quick_probe_03\\\\n    role: evidence\\\\n    result: Vendi -0.28%, converges to 107.25\\\\n  - exp_id: carr_baseline_no_curation_01\\\\n    role: baseline\\\\n    result: Vendi +3.1%\\\\n  depends_on:\\\\n  - D006\\\\n- id: D008\\\\n  date: 2026-05-16 20:37 UTC\\\\n  summary: Full-FT K=4 P1 FAIL (Vendi +0.9%)，LR underfitting 是根因。立即跑 LR=2e-5 Full-FT\\\\n    验证\\\\n  rationale: Full-FT K=4 probe Vendi 反而上升 +0.9%，与 baseline 趋势一致。Training loss 仅降 12%\\\\n    (vs LoRA 38%)，grad_norm 6-10，明确 underfitting。LR=5e-6 对 0.6B full-FT 太保守——模型没有充分学习\\\\n    curated data 的分布特征。提高 LR 到 2e-5 是成本最低的验证：如果模型真正 overfit 到 quality-curated 的窄分布上，应该能观察到\\\\n    diversity 下降。这是诊断梯的最后一次低成本尝试。\\\\n  related_experiments:\\\\n  - exp_id: carr_fullft_k4_probe_03\\\\n    role: evidence\\\\n    result: Vendi +0.9%, loss only 12% drop = underfitting\\\\n  depends_on:\\\\n  - D007\\\\n- id: D009\\\\n  date: 2026-05-17 00:27 UTC\\\\n  summary: P1 5/5 全部 FAIL，核心假设证伪：curation 不导致 diversity collapse（当前设置下）\\\\n  rationale: 5 种配置（K=4/K=8 LoRA, Full-FT LR=5e-6/2e-5, baseline）系统性验证：即使模型 loss 降\\\\n    80%（激进学习 curated 分布），Vendi Score 仍上升。LoRA 下 3pp gap 经 Full-FT 证实为 capacity artifact。在\\\\n    Qwen3-0.6B + 2200 prompts + K=4-8 + T=3 下，reward-guided curation 无法造成可观测的 diversity\\\\n    collapse。等待用户决策 pivot 方向。\\\\n  related_experiments:\\\\n  - exp_id: carr_quick_probe_03\\\\n    role: K=4 LoRA\\\\n    result: Vendi -0.27%, FAIL\\\\n  - exp_id: carr_quick_probe_k8_02\\\\n    role: K=8 LoRA\\\\n    result: Vendi -0.03%, FAIL\\\\n  - exp_id: carr_fullft_k4_probe_03\\\\n    role: Full-FT LR=5e-6\\\\n    result: Vendi +0.91%, FAIL\\\\n  - exp_id: carr_fullft_lr2e5_probe_01\\\\n    role: Full-FT LR=2e-5\\\\n    result: Vendi +0.68%, FAIL\\\\n  - exp_id: carr_baseline_no_curation_01\\\\n    role: baseline\\\\n    result: Vendi +3.13%\\\\n  depends_on:\\\\n  - D008\\\\n  - D007\\\\n  - D006\\\\n  - D005\\\\n  - D004\\\\n- id: D010\\\\n  date: 2026-05-17 01:23 UTC\\\\n  summary: '采用 C→D fallback pipeline: 24h 内验证 CARR Pareto optimizer (Direction C)，失败则切\\\\n    negative result paper (Direction D)'\\\\n  rationale: Reviewer 独立评估排序 C>D>E>A>B。Direction C 不依赖 collapse 存在，只需 CARR outperform\\\\n    fixed-weight，与现有代码基础兼容。Direction D 作为 fallback 数据已全部就绪。24h 决策窗口 (May 18 早) 兼顾试错空间和\\\\n    DDL 安全边际。K=16 结果对两条路径均有用。\\\\n  related_experiments:\\\\n  - exp_id: carr_fullft_lr2e5_probe_01\\\\n    role: P1 最终诊断\\\\n    result: Vendi +0.68%, FAIL\\\\n  - exp_id: carr_fullft_lr2e5_k16_probe_01\\\\n    role: 极端选择压力诊断\\\\n    result: RUNNING\\\\n  depends_on:\\\\n  - D009\\\\n- id: D011\\\\n  date: 2026-05-17 03:11 UTC\\\\n  summary: 'Direction C FAIL (CARR dry-run 99.3% overlap)，切 Direction D: negative\\\\n    result empirical study'\\\\n  rationale: CARR dry-run 证明 proxy scores 三维度高度相关 (r=0.63-0.77)，adaptive weights 几乎不改变\\\\n    selection (overlap 99.3%)。Direction C 的前提\\\\\\\"CARR 能 outperform fixed-weight\\\\\\\"不成立。Direction\\\\n    D 是唯一可行路径：数据完整、story 清晰（\\\\\\\"curation doesn't cause collapse under standard conditions\\\\\\\"）、7\\\\n    天可完成。竞品论文 5/5 真实，related work 定位无风险。K=16 仍在跑作为 bonus data point。\\\\n  related_experiments:\\\\n  - exp_id: carr_fullft_lr2e5_k16_probe_01\\\\n    role: K=16 boundary probe\\\\n    result: RUNNING\\\\n  - exp_id: carr_fullft_lr2e5_probe_01\\\\n    role: P1 final diagnostic\\\\n    result: Vendi +0.68%, FAIL\\\\n  depends_on:\\\\n  - D010\\\\n- id: D012\\\\n  date: 2026-05-17 09:46 UTC\\\\n  summary: K=16 P1 FAIL (Vendi -0.19%)，确认 Outline A：8 配置全部无 semantic collapse\\\\n  rationale: K=16 (6.25% 保留率) 是最极端的 selection pressure 测试。Vendi 仅变化 -0.19%，落在其他条件\\\\n    [-0.28%, +0.91%] 范围内。训练 loss 收敛更深 (0.097) 但语义多样性不受影响。至此 8/8 配置均无 semantic collapse，论文走\\\\n    Outline A (systematic negative result + metric-dependent collapse)。\\\\n  related_experiments:\\\\n  - exp_id: carr_fullft_lr2e5_k16_probe_01\\\\n    role: K=16 极端选择压力\\\\n    result: Vendi -0.19%, no collapse\\\\n  depends_on:\\\\n  - D011\\\\n- id: D013\\\\n  date: 2026-05-17 10:49 UTC\\\\n  summary: Format collapse 因果归因修正：从\\\\\\\"curation causes\\\\\\\"改为\\\\\\\"iterative self-training causes,\\\\n    K amplifies\\\\\\\"\\\\n  rationale: C1 vs C5 同 K=4 对比显示 format 退化幅度几乎完全相同（opening entropy 均 -0.75 bits，near-dup\\\\n    rate 差异仅 1.2pp）。原论文用 C2(K=8) vs C5(K=4) 混淆了 K 效应和 curation 效应。正确归因：format collapse\\\\n    是 iterative self-training 固有属性，K 是放大因子，curation 本身不额外导致 format collapse。这是更强的发现——完全为\\\\n    reward curation 正名。\\\\n  related_experiments:\\\\n  - exp_id: c1-regen-v2\\\\n    role: evidence\\\\n    result: C1 format metrics ≈ C5 format metrics at same K=4\\\\n  - exp_id: carr_quick_probe_k8_02\\\\n    role: contrast\\\\n    result: C2 K=8 shows stronger format collapse due to higher K, not curation\\\\n  depends_on:\\\\n  - D012\\\\n  - D009\\\\n- id: D014\\\\n  date: 2026-05-17 14:26 UTC\\\\n  summary: 保留叙事顺序（semantic→format→reversal），不采纳 Reviewer 建议的 format-first 重排\\\\n  rationale: '论文核心叙事弧是 Fear→Surprise→Understanding。format collapse 的新颖性通过\\\\\\\"semantic\\\\n    没问题→但 format 有问题\\\\\\\"的悬念反转放大，重排为 format-first 会削弱读者的 surprise 体验。贡献已精简为 3 条（合并 K amplifier\\\\n    + measurement 进 #2），结构足够紧凑。'\\\\n  related_experiments: []\\\\n  depends_on:\\\\n  - D013\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 8477, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_013w4D8h5fQdQZT973z4cAnC","session_id":"2b1f599e-fc53-49d6-adbf-f2a150d3d4f3","uuid":"a7aa00a6-a33e-4182-94ef-ec94ba762d45","timestamp":"2026-05-17T14:43:14.875Z"}
