{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01FyxpaqB4ffcreT69JToQV3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015rJmhL49MScn38LkczgN2k","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/collapse_aware_reward_rebalancing/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260517T135058/median/paper.tex","limit":100},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10067,"cache_read_input_tokens":41537,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":10067,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0171YWRYW2pbFasaRYcnpYd1","session_id":"2b1f599e-fc53-49d6-adbf-f2a150d3d4f3","uuid":"933f4acc-d2ea-41a0-a4f1-7dc2ed9ff56e"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015rJmhL49MScn38LkczgN2k","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n7\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n8\t\\usepackage{array}\n9\t\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n10\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n11\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n12\t\\usepackage{caption}\n13\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n14\t\n15\t\\newcommand{\\method}{CARR}\n16\t\\newcommand{\\vendi}{Vendi Score}\n17\t\n18\t\\title{The Resilience of Output Diversity Under Iterative Reward-Based Curation:\\\\A Systematic Empirical Study}\n19\t\n20\t\\author{Anonymous}\n21\t\\date{}\n22\t\n23\t\\begin{document}\n24\t\\maketitle\n25\t\n26\t%% ============================================================\n27\t%% ABSTRACT\n28\t%% ============================================================\n29\t\\begin{abstract}\n30\tIterative self-consuming training, where a language model is fine-tuned on its own filtered outputs across multiple generations, is widely feared to cause diversity collapse. We present a controlled empirical study across 10 configurations spanning two model scales (Qwen3-0.6B, Qwen2.5-1.5B), three training regimes (LoRA, Full Fine-Tuning with low and high learning rates), and selection pressures from K=4 to K=16. Our experiments yield three findings. First, semantic diversity measured by Vendi Score and nearest-neighbor distance is robust across all configurations, even under extreme selection pressure (K=16, 6.25\\% retention) where training loss drops 53\\%. Second, format homogenization (measured by opening-pattern entropy and near-duplicate start rates) is real but primarily driven by the iterative training process itself rather than curation; at 0.6B scale, curated and random selection produce identical format degradation, while at 1.5B scale curation acts as a secondary amplifier (0.35-bit gap). Third, at 0.6B scale the apparent diversity suppression under LoRA is a capacity artifact: under Full Fine-Tuning, curation's effect reverses from $-3.4$pp suppression to $+0.86$pp promotion. These results suggest that practitioners can safely apply reward-based curation without semantic diversity safeguards, but should monitor format-level metrics independently.\n31\t\\end{abstract}\n32\t\n33\t%% ============================================================\n34\t%% 1. INTRODUCTION\n35\t%% ============================================================\n36\t\\section{Introduction}\n37\t\n38\tThe practice of iteratively training language models on their own outputs, filtered through reward functions, has become central to alignment pipelines such as RLHF and iterative DPO. A persistent concern is that repeated selection of high-reward outputs progressively narrows the model's output distribution, leading to diversity collapse. Theoretical analyses by Shumailov et al.\\ (2024) and Alemohammad et al.\\ (2024) show that model collapse can occur under self-consuming training loops, and practitioners routinely implement diversity-preservation mechanisms to guard against this risk.\n39\t\n40\tYet the empirical evidence for diversity collapse under \\emph{reward-based} curation specifically remains thin. Most studies conflate three distinct phenomena: semantic narrowing of content, format homogenization of surface patterns, and training-method artifacts that masquerade as curation effects. Without controlled experiments that isolate these factors, the field risks over-engineering solutions to a problem that may not exist in the form assumed.\n41\t\n42\tWe address this gap with a systematic study across 10 experimental configurations. We train Qwen3-0.6B under 8 setups (varying curation strategy, selection pressure K, training method, and learning rate) for 3 self-consuming generations, and extend to Qwen2.5-1.5B under 2 setups for 5 generations. Each configuration includes a matched random-selection control to isolate the causal contribution of reward-based curation from the inherent dynamics of iterative self-training.\n43\t\n44\tOur study makes four contributions:\n45\t\\begin{enumerate}\n46\t    \\item We demonstrate that semantic diversity is resilient across all tested configurations, including extreme K=16 selection (6.25\\% retention) with 53\\% training loss reduction.\n47\t    \\item We show that format collapse is primarily an inherent property of iterative self-training, with curation acting as a scale-dependent secondary amplifier rather than the primary cause.\n48\t    \\item We identify a LoRA capacity artifact that creates a spurious negative signal for curation's effect on diversity, and show that under Full Fine-Tuning the effect reverses direction.\n49\t    \\item We propose two complementary metrics (opening-pattern entropy and near-duplicate start rate) that capture format homogenization invisible to standard embedding-based diversity measures.\n50\t\\end{enumerate}\n51\t\n52\t%% ============================================================\n53\t%% 2. RELATED WORK\n54\t%% ============================================================\n55\t\\section{Related Work}\n56\t\n57\t\\paragraph{Model Collapse in Self-Consuming Loops.}\n58\tShumailov et al.\\ (2024) demonstrated theoretically and empirically that iterative training on model-generated data can lead to progressive distribution narrowing. Alemohammad et al.\\ (2024) extended this analysis to generative image models, showing that even partial replacement of real data with synthetic data degrades diversity over generations. Our work differs by focusing specifically on reward-based curation rather than unconstrained self-consumption, and by separating semantic from format-level effects.\n59\t\n60\t\\paragraph{Diversity in Alignment Training.}\n61\tKirk et al.\\ (2024) studied diversity loss in RLHF pipelines, finding that reward optimization reduces lexical variety. However, their analysis did not control for the iterative component separately from the reward-selection component. Our controlled same-K comparison (curated vs.\\ random with identical data volumes) isolates the curation effect and finds it negligible for semantic diversity.\n62\t\n63\t\\paragraph{LoRA and Training Capacity.}\n64\tHu et al.\\ (2022) introduced LoRA as a parameter-efficient fine-tuning method. Biderman et al.\\ (2024) showed that LoRA can produce qualitatively different training dynamics compared to full fine-tuning. Our finding that LoRA creates an artificial diversity-suppression signal for curation extends this line of work to the iterative self-training setting.\n65\t\n66\t%% ============================================================\n67\t%% 3. METHOD\n68\t%% ============================================================\n69\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n70\t\n71\t\\subsection{Pipeline Overview}\n72\t\n73\tEach experimental configuration follows an iterative self-consuming loop:\n74\t\\begin{enumerate}\n75\t    \\item \\textbf{Generate}: Sample $N$ outputs from the current model checkpoint using a fixed prompt set (2,200 prompts).\n76\t    \\item \\textbf{Score}: Evaluate outputs using a reward model to obtain quality scores.\n77\t    \\item \\textbf{Select}: Apply top-K curation (retain top $1/K$ fraction by reward) or random selection (retain same fraction without reward ordering).\n78\t    \\item \\textbf{Train}: Fine-tune the model on selected outputs for one epoch.\n79\t    \\item \\textbf{Repeat}: Use the updated model as the starting point for the next generation.\n80\t\\end{enumerate}\n81\t\n82\t\\subsection{Configurations}\n83\t\n84\tWe study 10 configurations organized along four axes:\n85\t\n86\t\\paragraph{Model Scale.} Qwen3-0.6B (8 configurations, 3 generations) and Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct (2 configurations, 5 generations).\n87\t\n88\t\\paragraph{Training Method.} LoRA (rank=16, updating $<$1\\% of parameters) and Full Fine-Tuning (all parameters trainable).\n89\t\n90\t\\paragraph{Selection Pressure.} K $\\in \\{4, 8, 16\\}$, corresponding to retention rates of 25\\%, 12.5\\%, and 6.25\\%.\n91\t\n92\t\\paragraph{Curation Strategy.} Reward-based top-K selection (curated) versus random selection maintaining the same data volume (random control).\n93\t\n94\t\\paragraph{Learning Rate.} For Full-FT: high LR ($2 \\times 10^{-5}$) and low LR ($5 \\times 10^{-6}$).\n95\t\n96\t\\subsection{Metrics}\n97\t\n98\tWe measure diversity at two levels:\n99\t\n100\t\\paragraph{Semantic Diversity.}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0171YWRYW2pbFasaRYcnpYd1","session_id":"2b1f599e-fc53-49d6-adbf-f2a150d3d4f3","uuid":"42f2afa0-ab96-4f9b-82fb-f8ef88fb756c","timestamp":"2026-05-17T14:43:20.942Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a2b9968546496cfca","tool_use_id":"toolu_013w4D8h5fQdQZT973z4cAnC","description":"Reading docs/paper/results.tex","usage":{"total_tokens":36579,"tool_uses":13,"duration_ms":25941},"last_tool_name":"Read","uuid":"bd213079-6f61-4316-b1a5-9761d2e77c72","session_id":"2b1f599e-fc53-49d6-adbf-f2a150d3d4f3"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01CZ1sstNpu5UeHQKEcpytGe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015yjhnX2qeUmCsxCCo6ksdi","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/collapse_aware_reward_rebalancing/docs/paper/results.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3685,"cache_read_input_tokens":32605,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3685,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":73,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_013w4D8h5fQdQZT973z4cAnC","session_id":"2b1f599e-fc53-49d6-adbf-f2a150d3d4f3","uuid":"9d853636-d848-403f-b18c-acb4080463a2"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015yjhnX2qeUmCsxCCo6ksdi","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t% Section 4: Results\n2\t% Note: \\section{} header is in main.tex\n3\t\n4\t\\subsection{No Semantic Diversity Collapse}\n5\t\\label{sec:no_collapse}\n6\t\n7\tReward-based curation does not cause semantic diversity collapse over three generations of iterative self-training. Table~\\ref{tab:vendi_trajectory} reports Vendi Scores for all conditions across generations. Among the five curated configurations (C1--C4, C8), relative changes from Gen~0 to Gen~2 range from $-$0.27\\% to $+$0.91\\%, all within $\\pm$1\\%. This stability persists across every factor we vary: adaptation method (LoRA vs.\\ Full-FT), candidate pool size ($K \\in \\{4, 8, 16\\}$), and learning rate (5e-6 vs.\\ 2e-5). C8, with the strongest selection pressure ($K{=}16$, 6.25\\% retention), produces a change of only $-$0.19\\%. Both Full-FT curated conditions (C3, C4) exhibit monotonically increasing Vendi Scores, while LoRA conditions remain flat or fluctuate transiently before recovery.\n8\t\n9\t\\begin{table}[t]\n10\t\\centering\n11\t\\small\n12\t\\caption{Vendi Score across three self-training generations. $\\Delta$ denotes relative change from Gen~0 to Gen~2. All curated conditions remain within $\\pm$1\\%, while random baselines show larger positive drift.}\n13\t\\label{tab:vendi_trajectory}\n14\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}llcccc@{}}\n15\t\\toprule\n16\t& Config & Gen~0 & Gen~1 & Gen~2 & $\\Delta$ (\\%) \\\\\n17\t\\midrule\n18\t\\multicolumn{6}{@{}l}{\\textit{Curated (ArmoRM top-1)}} \\\\\n19\tC1 & LoRA, $K{=}4$ & 107.54 & 106.73 & 107.25 & $-$0.27 \\\\\n20\tC2 & LoRA, $K{=}8$ & 107.21 & 107.26 & 107.25 & $+$0.04 \\\\\n21\tC3 & Full-FT, 5e-6 & 107.10 & 107.61 & 108.07 & $+$0.91 \\\\\n22\tC4 & Full-FT, 2e-5 & 107.81 & 108.03 & 108.54 & $+$0.68 \\\\\n23\tC8 & Full-FT, $K{=}16$ & 107.32 & 106.73 & 107.11 & $-$0.19 \\\\\n24\t\\midrule\n25\t\\multicolumn{6}{@{}l}{\\textit{Random baseline (1-of-$K$)}} \\\\\n26\tC5 & LoRA, $K{=}4$ & 105.42 & 108.21 & 108.72 & $+$3.13 \\\\\n27\tC6 & Full-FT, 5e-6 & 107.17 & 107.87 & 108.64 & $+$1.37 \\\\\n28\tC7 & Full-FT, 2e-5 & 108.08 & 107.85 & 108.13 & $+$0.05 \\\\\n29\t\\bottomrule\n30\t\\end{tabular}\n31\t\\end{table}\n32\t\n33\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n34\t\\centering\n35\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_vendi_trajectories.pdf}\n36\t\\caption{Vendi Score trajectories across three self-training generations for all 8 configurations. Curated conditions (solid lines) remain within $\\pm$1\\% of Gen~0, while random baselines (dashed) show larger positive drift.}\n37\t\\label{fig:vendi_trajectories}\n38\t\\end{figure}\n39\t\n40\tFigure~\\ref{fig:vendi_trajectories} visualizes these trajectories. Secondary metrics corroborate this result. Self-BLEU stays below 0.01 for all curated conditions (absolute values 0.002--0.009), and Self-BLEU-4 computed over full training corpora confirms this pattern (all conditions $<$0.016; Appendix~\\ref{app:lexical_diversity}). Nearest-Neighbor Distance remains between 0.70 and 0.71 with no systematic contraction. These results contrast with predictions from the model collapse literature \\cite{placeholder_shumailov, placeholder_alemohammad}. The critical difference is that each generation draws fresh stochastic samples ($T{=}0.7$) from the current model, and the prompt set provides a constant external anchor.\n41\t\n42\t\\subsection{Relative Suppression, Not Absolute Collapse}\n43\t\\label{sec:suppression}\n44\t\n45\tCurated conditions do not collapse, but they grow diversity more slowly than matched random baselines. C5 and C6 show Vendi Score increases of $+$3.13\\% and $+$1.37\\% (Table~\\ref{tab:vendi_trajectory}), compared to $-$0.27\\% and $+$0.91\\% for their curated counterparts C1 and C3. This distinction matters: studies reporting that reward filtering reduces diversity relative to unfiltered baselines \\cite{placeholder_kirk} are sometimes read as evidence of degeneration. Our data show that a curated model holding steady has not collapsed, even if an uncurated model drifted above it. Evaluating curation requires comparing each model against its own starting point, not against a diverging uncurated trajectory.\n46\t\n47\t\\subsection{Direction Reversal Across Training Regimes}\n48\t\\label{sec:direction_reversal}\n49\t\n50\tThe curation gap (curated $\\Delta$ minus random $\\Delta$, in percentage points) reverses sign as training capacity increases. Under LoRA (C1 vs.\\ C5), the gap is $-$3.40~pp. Under Full-FT at 5e-6 (C3 vs.\\ C6), it contracts to $-$0.46~pp. Under Full-FT at 2e-5 (C4 vs.\\ C7), it reverses to $+$0.63~pp: curation now promotes diversity (Figure~\\ref{fig:direction_reversal}).\n51\t\n52\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n53\t\\centering\n54\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_direction_reversal.pdf}\n55\t\\caption{Curation gap (curated $\\Delta$ minus random $\\Delta$, in percentage points) across training regimes. The gap transitions from negative (curation suppresses diversity) under LoRA to positive (curation promotes diversity) under Full-FT with higher learning rate.}\n56\t\\label{fig:direction_reversal}\n57\t\\end{figure}\n58\t\n59\t\\begin{table}[t]\n60\t\\centering\n61\t\\small\n62\t\\caption{Training loss per generation. LoRA conditions converge to similar final loss regardless of curation strategy. C8 ($K{=}16$) reaches the lowest Gen~2 loss (0.097).}\n63\t\\label{tab:training_loss}\n64\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}llccc@{}}\n65\t\\toprule\n66\t& Config & Gen~0 & Gen~1 & Gen~2 \\\\\n67\t\\midrule\n68\tC1 & LoRA, curated & 0.413 & 0.240 & 0.166 \\\\\n69\tC2 & LoRA ($K{=}8$), curated & 0.411 & 0.239 & 0.168 \\\\\n70\tC5 & LoRA, random & 0.407 & 0.224 & 0.152 \\\\\n71\t\\midrule\n72\tC3 & Full-FT 5e-6, curated & 0.461 & 0.341 & 0.285 \\\\\n73\tC6 & Full-FT 5e-6, random & 0.461 & 0.335 & 0.277 \\\\\n74\t\\midrule\n75\tC4 & Full-FT 2e-5, curated & 0.380 & 0.212 & 0.140 \\\\\n76\tC8 & Full-FT 2e-5, $K{=}16$ & 0.206 & 0.129 & \\textbf{0.097} \\\\\n77\tC7 & Full-FT 2e-5, random & 0.377 & 0.199 & 0.132 \\\\\n78\t\\bottomrule\n79\t\\end{tabular}\n80\t\\end{table}\n81\t\n82\tTraining loss trajectories (Table~\\ref{tab:training_loss}) explain this reversal. Under LoRA, curated and random conditions converge to similar final loss (C1: 0.166, C5: 0.152). The rank-16 adapter's low-dimensional bottleneck limits how far any signal can reshape the output distribution. Random data, unconstrained by reward consistency, permits slightly more distributional drift within this bottleneck, producing the observed gap. Full-FT removes this constraint. At lr=2e-5, reward-selected data provides varied high-quality completions across topics that translate into greater semantic diversity when the model can fully absorb the curated distribution. The progression from LoRA through conservative Full-FT to aggressive Full-FT traces a continuum from parameter-limited to parameter-sufficient training.\n83\t\n84\tThis pattern is consistent with a capacity-mediated mechanism: LoRA's rank-16 bottleneck limits how much distributional structure the model can absorb, and the apparent suppression effect disappears when this constraint is removed. The practical implication is that experiments conducted exclusively with parameter-efficient methods may reach conclusions about curation's diversity impact that do not generalize to full fine-tuning.\n85\t\n86\t\\subsection{Quality-Diversity Non-Tradeoff}\n87\t\\label{sec:quality_diversity}\n88\t\n89\tCuration produces neither a quality gain large enough to motivate its use nor a diversity cost large enough to discourage it. Mean ArmoRM scores at Gen~2 range from 0.1096 (C5) to 0.1149 (C8), a spread of less than 5\\%. All conditions show mild quality decreases from Gen~0 to Gen~2 ($-$0.3\\% to $-$1.3\\%; full per-generation scores in Table~\\ref{tab:quality_scores}). The absence of meaningful quality improvement reflects saturation in the candidate quality distribution: the score gap between top-1 and average candidate is approximately 0.04, too narrow for the fine-tuned model to learn a meaningfully different quality level. The combined picture is that reward-based curation operates in a narrow effect regime at this scale: it does not collapse diversity, does not measurably improve quality, and its relative diversity effect depends more on training capacity than on curation strategy.\n90\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_013w4D8h5fQdQZT973z4cAnC","session_id":"2b1f599e-fc53-49d6-adbf-f2a150d3d4f3","uuid":"7f431dd8-30cd-4612-ba14-c46237483ad0","timestamp":"2026-05-17T14:43:24.618Z"}
