{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01D9bZyNbNZyJ9S2yCrrLfsY","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n7\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n8\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n9\t\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n10\t\\usepackage[numbers]{natbib}\n11\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n12\t\\usepackage{caption}\n13\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n14\t\\usepackage{array}\n15\t\\usepackage{tabularx}\n16\t\n17\t\\definecolor{safegreen}{rgb}{0.0,0.5,0.0}\n18\t\\definecolor{dangerred}{rgb}{0.7,0.0,0.0}\n19\t\n20\t\\title{When Does Curation Become Dangerous? \\\\\n21\tScale-Dependent Diversity Collapse in Iterative Reward-Based Training}\n22\t\n23\t\\author{Anonymous}\n24\t\\date{}\n25\t\n26\t\\begin{document}\n27\t\\maketitle\n28\t\n29\t% ============================================================\n30\t% ABSTRACT\n31\t% ============================================================\n32\t\\begin{abstract}\n33\tIterative self-consuming training with reward-based curation risks compounding diversity loss across generations. We present a systematic empirical study of this phenomenon across eight configurations of Qwen3-0.6B (varying selection pressure $K$, training method, and learning rate) over three generations, complemented by a cross-scale probe on Qwen2.5-1.5B over five generations. At 0.6B parameters, semantic diversity measured by Vendi Score remains stable across all configurations, including extreme $K{=}16$ (6.25\\% retention) where training loss drops by 53\\%. We find that format homogenization (opening entropy decline, near-duplicate increase) is real but inherent to iterative self-training rather than caused by curation, as shown by controlled same-$K$ comparison between curated and random selection. We also identify a capacity artifact: the apparent diversity suppression under LoRA reverses to mild diversity promotion under full fine-tuning, following a monotonic gradient across three capacity regimes. At 1.5B parameters, the same curation protocol produces a qualitatively different outcome: Vendi Score declines by 4.23\\% over five generations with monotonically shrinking nearest-neighbor distances, rising self-BLEU, and falling perplexity, constituting a clear diversity collapse signature. This contrast reveals a scale-dependent phase transition: curation that is harmless below 1B parameters becomes actively destructive above it. We discuss the implications for practitioners and the limitations imposed by our incomplete 1.5B control condition.\n34\t\\end{abstract}\n35\t\n36\t% ============================================================\n37\t% 1. INTRODUCTION\n38\t% ============================================================\n39\t\\section{Introduction}\n40\t\n41\tSelf-consuming generative processes, where a model is iteratively fine-tuned on its own outputs, have become a practical component of alignment pipelines that alternate generation, scoring, and retraining. A persistent concern is that such loops compound selection bias: if each generation selects for high-reward outputs, the training distribution may progressively narrow, eventually collapsing into a low-diversity mode that scores well but lacks the variation needed for robust downstream use.\n42\t\n43\tThis concern is grounded in both theory and recent empirical work. Model collapse under iterative self-training has been documented across image generators and language models, with diversity loss accelerating when only partial data is retained across generations. Reward-based curation, which retains only the top-$K$ scoring responses per prompt, applies additional selection pressure on top of the iterative training loop, making collapse appear even more likely.\n44\t\n45\tYet the interaction between curation intensity, model capacity, and iterative depth remains poorly characterized. Prior work has largely studied collapse in settings without explicit reward-based selection, or has focused on single configurations without systematic ablation. It is unclear whether curation is a primary driver of collapse, a neutral bystander, or even a protective factor, and whether the answer depends on model scale.\n46\t\n47\tWe address this gap with a controlled empirical study. Our primary analysis covers eight configurations of Qwen3-0.6B spanning selection pressure ($K \\in \\{4, 8, 16\\}$), training method (LoRA vs. full fine-tuning), learning rate (5e-6 vs. 2e-5), and the presence or absence of curation, each run for three iterative generations. We complement this with a cross-scale probe: the same protocol applied to Qwen2.5-1.5B for five generations.\n48\t\n49\tThe results reveal a striking scale-dependent pattern. At 0.6B, semantic diversity is completely stable across all eight configurations. Even under extreme selection pressure ($K{=}16$, 6.25\\% retention), Vendi Score changes by less than 0.3\\%. Format homogenization does occur but is equally present in curated and random conditions, identifying it as an inherent property of iterative self-training. A capacity artifact further complicates interpretation: under LoRA, curation appears to suppress diversity relative to random selection, but this reversal disappears and flips sign under full fine-tuning, following a monotonic gradient across three capacity regimes.\n50\t\n51\tAt 1.5B, the picture changes fundamentally. Vendi Score declines by 4.23\\% over five generations, nearest-neighbor distance drops monotonically from 0.712 to 0.678, self-BLEU nearly doubles, and perplexity falls steadily, constituting a multi-metric collapse signature absent from any 0.6B configuration.\n52\t\n53\tThis contrast constitutes a scale-dependent phase transition for curation-induced collapse. We note an important limitation: our 1.5B random baseline was terminated due to infrastructure constraints (CUDA out-of-memory), preventing causal attribution of the 1.5B collapse to curation versus scale-inherent iterative degradation. We discuss both interpretations and their practical implications.\n54\t\n55\tOur contributions are:\n56\t\\begin{enumerate}\n57\t    \\item \\textbf{Sub-1B resilience characterization}: a systematic eight-configuration study showing that reward-based curation does not cause semantic diversity collapse at the 0.6B scale, even under extreme selection pressure.\n58\t    \\item \\textbf{Scale-dependent phase transition}: identification of a qualitative shift between 0.6B (stable) and 1.5B (collapsing) under identical curation protocols, suggesting a critical scale threshold for curation risk.\n59\t    \\item \\textbf{Causal decomposition of format collapse}: controlled same-$K$ comparison demonstrating that format homogenization is inherent to iterative self-training, not caused by curation, with selection pressure $K$ as the severity amplifier (verified at 0.6B).\n60\t    \\item \\textbf{Capacity artifact identification}: discovery that LoRA's low-rank constraint creates a misleading diversity suppression signal, with a clean direction reversal under full fine-tuning across three capacity regimes.\n61\t\\end{enumerate}\n62\t\n63\t% ============================================================\n64\t% 2. RELATED WORK\n65\t% ============================================================\n66\t\\section{Related Work}\n67\t\n68\t\\paragraph{Model collapse in self-consuming loops.}\n69\tThe phenomenon of iterative self-training leading to distributional narrowing has been studied under several names. Shumailov et al. demonstrated that language models trained on their own outputs over multiple generations exhibit progressive quality degradation and mode collapse. Alemohammad et al. formalized self-consuming generative processes and showed that without fresh data injection, iterative training leads to distributional drift. Dohmatob et al. provided theoretical analysis showing that tail truncation in iterative fitting causes progressive loss of distributional support. Our work differs in that we explicitly study reward-based curation as the selection mechanism and find that its effect is scale-dependent.\n70\t\n71\t\\paragraph{Reward over-optimization in alignment.}\n72\tThe alignment literature has documented reward hacking and over-optimization, where models learn to exploit reward model weaknesses rather than genuinely improve. Gao et al. characterized the scaling behavior of reward over-optimization. Our 1.5B results, showing rising quality scores alongside falling diversity, are consistent with this pattern at the iterative training level.\n73\t\n74\t\\paragraph{Diversity metrics for generative models.}\n75\tVendi Score, based on matrix eigenvalue entropy, has been proposed as a reference-free diversity metric. Self-BLEU measures surface-level repetition. We show that embedding-based metrics like Vendi Score are blind to format-level homogenization, and propose complementary metrics (opening pattern entropy, near-duplicate start rate) that capture this dimension.\n76\t\n77\t\\paragraph{LoRA and training capacity.}\n78\tLow-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) constrains parameter updates to a low-rank subspace. While computationally efficient, this constraint limits the model's ability to shift its output distribution. We show that this capacity limitation creates artifacts in diversity measurements, producing misleading signals about curation's effect.\n79\t\n80\t% ============================================================"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0171YWRYW2pbFasaRYcnpYd1","session_id":"2b1f599e-fc53-49d6-adbf-f2a150d3d4f3","uuid":"40d38111-9546-4fa3-8c25-7ede51c4ebe5","timestamp":"2026-05-17T14:43:27.421Z"}
