{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0171YWRYW2pbFasaRYcnpYd1","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of the paper under review. Let me compile my review.\n\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\nThis paper studies diversity collapse under iterative reward-based curation by running 8 configurations on Qwen3-0.6B (3 generations) and a cross-scale probe on Qwen2.5-1.5B (5 generations). The 0.6B experiments are well-controlled and show no semantic collapse even under extreme K=16 curation, while format homogenization occurs equally under curated and random selection. The 1.5B probe reveals a 4.23% Vendi Score decline with multiple co-occurring collapse indicators, but the critical random baseline was killed by CUDA OOM, leaving the paper's central \"scale-dependent phase transition\" claim without causal support.\n\n### Strengths\nS1. The 0.6B experimental grid is well-designed with proper controls. The factorial structure isolating adaptation method, selection pressure K, learning rate, and curation strategy enables clean causal attribution. The matched curated/random pairs (C1/C5, C3/C6, C4/C7) are exactly the right controls.\n\nS2. The identification of metric-dependent collapse is a genuine contribution. The demonstration that embedding-based metrics (Vendi Score, Self-BLEU) miss format-level homogenization visible to humans is useful and practically actionable. The nearly identical format degradation between C1 (curated) and C5 (random) at 0.6B is a clean separation of causes.\n\nS3. The capacity artifact finding (direction reversal across LoRA, Full-FT low-LR, Full-FT high-LR) is supported by a three-point monotonic gradient, making it difficult to dismiss as noise. This is a methodologically important warning for the community: LoRA-only evaluations of curation effects can produce misleading conclusions.\n\nS4. The paper is commendably honest about its limitations. The Discussion section explicitly presents both Hypothesis A (curation-caused) and Hypothesis B (scale-inherent) for the 1.5B collapse, and the Limitations section enumerates the missing control problem and K x scale interaction gap clearly.\n\nS5. The K=16 stress test (C8) is a strong robustness check: 6.25% retention rate with 53% loss drop and essentially zero Vendi change strengthens the 0.6B stability finding.\n\n### Weaknesses\nW1. [CRITICAL] The missing 1.5B random baseline fatally undermines the paper's central contribution. The title frames this as a \"scale-dependent\" phase transition, but with n=1 at 1.5B (curated only, no control), it is impossible to distinguish three equally plausible explanations: (a) curation causes collapse at scale, (b) iterative self-training itself causes collapse at scale regardless of curation, (c) the Qwen2.5 architecture is more susceptible than Qwen3 irrespective of scale. The paper's most interesting claim has its weakest evidence. A phase transition requires at minimum demonstrating that the control condition behaves differently at the two scales.\n\nW2. [CRITICAL] The 0.6B and 1.5B experiments use different model families (Qwen3-0.6B vs. Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct), confounding model scale with architecture, pretraining data, instruction-tuning methodology, and tokenizer differences. The paper claims a \"scale-dependent\" transition, but the data equally support an \"architecture-dependent\" or \"pretraining-data-dependent\" interpretation. This is not just a limitation footnote; it invalidates the headline finding. To claim a scale effect, one must hold architecture constant (e.g., Qwen3-0.6B vs. Qwen3-1.7B, or Qwen2.5-0.5B vs. Qwen2.5-1.5B).\n\nW3. [MAJOR] No statistical significance testing or confidence intervals are reported for any metric. All diversity metrics are computed on 200 evaluation prompts (or 2,000 training prompts for format metrics) with a single run per configuration. Vendi Score changes of -0.28% or +0.91% are presented as meaningful distinctions, but without variance estimates or repeated runs, these could be within sampling noise. The curation gap computation (curated delta minus random delta) compounds the variance of two single-run estimates. The direction reversal claim (-3.41pp to +0.86pp) rests entirely on point estimates from unreplicated experiments.\n\nW4. [MAJOR] The \"incapacity vs. resilience\" alternative explanation is acknowledged but not resolved. The K=16 result paradoxically undermines the generalizability of 0.6B findings: if a model with 53% loss drop and 6.25% retention still shows zero diversity change, the model may simply be too small to exhibit the phenomenon. This means the 0.6B-scoped claims (format collapse separation, capacity artifact) may themselves be artifacts of studying a model below the relevant capacity threshold. The paper acknowledges this but still frames these as contributions rather than possibly vacuous observations about a regime where nothing interesting happens.\n\nW5. [MAJOR] The P1 collapse criterion (Vendi Score decline exceeding 2%) is arbitrary and never validated. Why 2% and not 1% or 5%? Is this threshold meaningful in terms of human-perceived diversity? The binary PASS/FAIL framework obscures the continuous nature of the data and gives false precision to the \"collapse detection\" narrative.\n\nW6. [MAJOR] Only 3 generations at 0.6B is very short for studying iterative collapse. The model collapse literature typically shows effects emerging over many more iterations. The paper's own 1.5B experiment runs 5 generations and shows progressive decline that would likely continue. Three generations may simply be insufficient for effects to manifest at 0.6B, especially given the \"incapacity\" alternative.\n\nW7. [MINOR] The format metrics (opening pattern entropy, near-duplicate start rate) are ad hoc and not validated against human judgments. While intuitively reasonable, there is no calibration showing that a 0.75-bit entropy drop corresponds to a perceptible increase in format homogeneity to human readers. The 50-character prefix threshold for near-duplicate detection is also arbitrary.\n\nW8. [MINOR] The quality metric uses the same ArmoRM model as the curation reward model, creating circular evaluation. The paper acknowledges reward saturation (score gap of ~0.04), but this circularity means \"quality stability\" is trivially expected when the model is trained to match the evaluator.\n\nW9. [MINOR] Table 1 is missing data (C6 nn-distance is \"---\", C7 quality is \"---\"). Incomplete tables in a submission suggest rushed execution.\n\n### Questions for Authors\nQ1. Have you attempted to run Qwen2.5-0.5B or Qwen3-1.7B under the same protocol to disentangle scale from architecture? Even one additional data point within the same model family would substantially strengthen the scale-dependent claim.\n\nQ2. What is the variance of Vendi Score across different random seeds for generation and training? If you ran C1 three times with different seeds, what range of Vendi deltas would you observe? Without this, the entire significance of <1% changes cannot be assessed.\n\nQ3. The curated 1.5B experiment completed but the random baseline OOM'd due to length distribution differences. This means the curated training data has systematically shorter sequences than a random sample. Could this implicit length filtering, rather than reward quality, be responsible for the different diversity dynamics? Have you measured the length distribution of curated vs. random selected data at 1.5B?\n\nQ4. For the direction reversal, C4 (Full-FT low-LR, curated) is labeled in Table 1 as \"Full-FT (lo)\" but no matched random baseline for this regime is presented in the paper's Tables. C6 in Table 1 is \"Full-FT (hi) random.\" Where is the Full-FT (lo) random configuration needed to compute the intermediate point of the three-regime gradient?\n\nQ5. Why was LoRA rank fixed at 16 for 0.6B and also used at 1.5B? At 1.5B, rank 16 represents a much smaller fraction of parameters (~0.5% vs ~1.67%), which means the capacity constraint is even tighter. Have you considered that the 1.5B collapse might be partly driven by LoRA rank being proportionally smaller?\n\n### Missing Experiments\nM1. [CRITICAL] Qwen2.5-1.5B LoRA K=4 random baseline (5 generations). This is the single most important missing experiment. Without it, the paper's headline finding is an uncontrolled observation. Even completing 3 of the 5 generations would provide substantial value.\n\nM2. [CRITICAL] Same-family scale comparison (either Qwen3 at a larger scale, or Qwen2.5 at a smaller scale) to separate the scale variable from the architecture variable. Without this, \"scale-dependent phase transition\" is an unsupported interpretation.\n\nM3. [MAJOR] Repeated runs (at least 3 seeds) for a subset of key configurations (e.g., C1, C5, C3, C6) to establish variance estimates and enable significance testing. Currently all claims rest on single-run point estimates.\n\nM4. [MAJOR] K > 4 at 1.5B scale (K=8 or K=16). The K x scale interaction is completely unexplored. If K=4 already produces collapse at 1.5B, K=16 could produce catastrophic collapse, which would be important to know.\n\nM5. [MINOR] Human evaluation of format diversity to validate the proposed format metrics. A small study asking annotators to judge whether response sets feel \"repetitive\" or \"diverse\" and correlating with the format metrics would substantially strengthen the measurement contribution.\n\n### Score: 3/6\n### Confidence: 4/5\n\nThe 0.6B factorial study is thorough and internally valid, and the format-vs-semantic metric separation is a useful methodological contribution. However, the paper's framing and title promise a scale-dependent finding that the evidence cannot support. The confound between model family and scale, the missing 1.5B control, the absence of any statistical testing, and the very real possibility that 0.6B is simply below the capacity threshold for the studied phenomenon collectively prevent acceptance. This work would benefit from completing the 1.5B random baseline, adding a same-family scale comparison, and reframing the contribution around what is actually well-supported: the 0.6B factorial analysis and the metric-dependent collapse observation."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a9a3cf6723338f7f9 (use SendMessage with to: 'a9a3cf6723338f7f9' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 95725\ntool_uses: 23\nduration_ms: 131520</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2b1f599e-fc53-49d6-adbf-f2a150d3d4f3","uuid":"3fc935bd-9e24-4ea2-b462-e13a48f53151","timestamp":"2026-05-17T14:44:50.491Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **methods and experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"When Does Curation Become Dangerous? Scale-Dependent Diversity Collapse in Iterative Reward-Based Training\"\n\nThe paper studies diversity collapse in iterative self-consuming training with reward-based curation. It runs 8 configurations on Qwen3-0.6B (varying K∈{4,8,16}, LoRA vs full-FT, LR, curated vs random) over 3 generations, plus a cross-scale probe on Qwen2.5-1.5B over 5 generations.\n\nKey findings:\n1. At 0.6B, semantic diversity (Vendi Score) is stable across all 8 configs, even K=16 with 53% loss drop.\n2. Format homogenization (opening entropy, near-dup rate) is inherent to iterative self-training, not curation-caused (shown by C1 curated ≈ C5 random at K=4).\n3. LoRA creates a capacity artifact: curation appears to suppress diversity under LoRA but reverses to mild promotion under full-FT, with monotonic gradient across 3 capacity regimes.\n4. At 1.5B, Vendi Score declines 4.23% over 5 gens with monotonic nn-distance decline, rising self-BLEU, falling perplexity = multi-metric collapse.\n5. 1.5B random baseline was killed 3x by CUDA OOM, so causal attribution (curation-caused vs scale-inherent) is impossible.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe imagined experiment list includes 10 plans:\n- plan_001-008: 0.6B configs (LoRA K=4 curated, LoRA K=8 curated, LoRA K=4 random, Full-FT K=4 curated hi-LR, Full-FT K=4 curated lo-LR, Full-FT K=4 random, Full-FT K=16, C1 regen for format comparison)\n- plan_009: Qwen2.5-1.5B LoRA K=4 curated (5 gens) — completed, shows collapse (Vendi -4.23%)\n- plan_010: Qwen2.5-1.5B LoRA K=4 random — KILLED during gen1 (CUDA OOM), only gen0 available\n\nKey context from the YAML:\n- The 0.6B \"resilience\" may actually be \"incapacity\" — the model is too small to drift. K=16 stress test (80% loss drop, still no diversity change) supports this alternative.\n- The 1.5B collapse cannot be causally separated from scale-inherent effects without the missing random baseline.\n- K × scale interaction is completely unexplored (only K=4 at 1.5B).\n- 0.6B and 1.5B use different model families (Qwen3 vs Qwen2.5), confounding scale and architecture.\n- The paper's original narrative was \"universal resilience\" but had to pivot to \"scale-conditional analysis\" after 1.5B results broke Claim 1.\n\n## Tables in the paper\n- Table 1: 8 configs at 0.6B, all P1=FAIL (no collapse). Vendi changes -0.28% to +1.13%.\n- Table 2: Vendi trajectory summary across scales. 0.6B stable, 1.5B collapsing.\n- Table 3: Format metrics. C1≈C5 on full-pool entropy drop (~-0.75 bits). K=8 amplifies to -1.13 bits.\n- Table 4: 1.5B trajectory over 5 gens. All diversity metrics decline monotonically, quality rises.\n- Table 5: Training loss dynamics. 0.6B K=16: 53% drop. 1.5B: 67% drop.\n\n## Your Review Task\nEvaluate the paper on:\n1. **Experimental design**: Are the experiments well-designed? Are controls adequate? Are there critical missing experiments?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Sample sizes, significance testing, reproducibility.\n3. **Claims vs evidence alignment**: Does each claim have sufficient supporting evidence? Are claims appropriately scoped?\n4. **Methodology**: Is the iterative training protocol sound? Are metrics appropriate?\n\nFor each issue, classify severity as CRITICAL / MAJOR / MINOR.\n\nOutput your review in this format:\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\nS1. ...\nS2. ...\n\n### Weaknesses\nW1. [SEVERITY] ...\nW2. [SEVERITY] ...\n\n### Questions for Authors\nQ1. ...\n\n### Missing Experiments\nM1. ...\n\n### Score: X/6\n### Confidence: X/5\n```\n","agentId":"a9a3cf6723338f7f9","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of the paper under review. Let me compile my review.\n\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\nThis paper studies diversity collapse under iterative reward-based curation by running 8 configurations on Qwen3-0.6B (3 generations) and a cross-scale probe on Qwen2.5-1.5B (5 generations). The 0.6B experiments are well-controlled and show no semantic collapse even under extreme K=16 curation, while format homogenization occurs equally under curated and random selection. The 1.5B probe reveals a 4.23% Vendi Score decline with multiple co-occurring collapse indicators, but the critical random baseline was killed by CUDA OOM, leaving the paper's central \"scale-dependent phase transition\" claim without causal support.\n\n### Strengths\nS1. The 0.6B experimental grid is well-designed with proper controls. The factorial structure isolating adaptation method, selection pressure K, learning rate, and curation strategy enables clean causal attribution. The matched curated/random pairs (C1/C5, C3/C6, C4/C7) are exactly the right controls.\n\nS2. The identification of metric-dependent collapse is a genuine contribution. The demonstration that embedding-based metrics (Vendi Score, Self-BLEU) miss format-level homogenization visible to humans is useful and practically actionable. The nearly identical format degradation between C1 (curated) and C5 (random) at 0.6B is a clean separation of causes.\n\nS3. The capacity artifact finding (direction reversal across LoRA, Full-FT low-LR, Full-FT high-LR) is supported by a three-point monotonic gradient, making it difficult to dismiss as noise. This is a methodologically important warning for the community: LoRA-only evaluations of curation effects can produce misleading conclusions.\n\nS4. The paper is commendably honest about its limitations. The Discussion section explicitly presents both Hypothesis A (curation-caused) and Hypothesis B (scale-inherent) for the 1.5B collapse, and the Limitations section enumerates the missing control problem and K x scale interaction gap clearly.\n\nS5. The K=16 stress test (C8) is a strong robustness check: 6.25% retention rate with 53% loss drop and essentially zero Vendi change strengthens the 0.6B stability finding.\n\n### Weaknesses\nW1. [CRITICAL] The missing 1.5B random baseline fatally undermines the paper's central contribution. The title frames this as a \"scale-dependent\" phase transition, but with n=1 at 1.5B (curated only, no control), it is impossible to distinguish three equally plausible explanations: (a) curation causes collapse at scale, (b) iterative self-training itself causes collapse at scale regardless of curation, (c) the Qwen2.5 architecture is more susceptible than Qwen3 irrespective of scale. The paper's most interesting claim has its weakest evidence. A phase transition requires at minimum demonstrating that the control condition behaves differently at the two scales.\n\nW2. [CRITICAL] The 0.6B and 1.5B experiments use different model families (Qwen3-0.6B vs. Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct), confounding model scale with architecture, pretraining data, instruction-tuning methodology, and tokenizer differences. The paper claims a \"scale-dependent\" transition, but the data equally support an \"architecture-dependent\" or \"pretraining-data-dependent\" interpretation. This is not just a limitation footnote; it invalidates the headline finding. To claim a scale effect, one must hold architecture constant (e.g., Qwen3-0.6B vs. Qwen3-1.7B, or Qwen2.5-0.5B vs. Qwen2.5-1.5B).\n\nW3. [MAJOR] No statistical significance testing or confidence intervals are reported for any metric. All diversity metrics are computed on 200 evaluation prompts (or 2,000 training prompts for format metrics) with a single run per configuration. Vendi Score changes of -0.28% or +0.91% are presented as meaningful distinctions, but without variance estimates or repeated runs, these could be within sampling noise. The curation gap computation (curated delta minus random delta) compounds the variance of two single-run estimates. The direction reversal claim (-3.41pp to +0.86pp) rests entirely on point estimates from unreplicated experiments.\n\nW4. [MAJOR] The \"incapacity vs. resilience\" alternative explanation is acknowledged but not resolved. The K=16 result paradoxically undermines the generalizability of 0.6B findings: if a model with 53% loss drop and 6.25% retention still shows zero diversity change, the model may simply be too small to exhibit the phenomenon. This means the 0.6B-scoped claims (format collapse separation, capacity artifact) may themselves be artifacts of studying a model below the relevant capacity threshold. The paper acknowledges this but still frames these as contributions rather than possibly vacuous observations about a regime where nothing interesting happens.\n\nW5. [MAJOR] The P1 collapse criterion (Vendi Score decline exceeding 2%) is arbitrary and never validated. Why 2% and not 1% or 5%? Is this threshold meaningful in terms of human-perceived diversity? The binary PASS/FAIL framework obscures the continuous nature of the data and gives false precision to the \"collapse detection\" narrative.\n\nW6. [MAJOR] Only 3 generations at 0.6B is very short for studying iterative collapse. The model collapse literature typically shows effects emerging over many more iterations. The paper's own 1.5B experiment runs 5 generations and shows progressive decline that would likely continue. Three generations may simply be insufficient for effects to manifest at 0.6B, especially given the \"incapacity\" alternative.\n\nW7. [MINOR] The format metrics (opening pattern entropy, near-duplicate start rate) are ad hoc and not validated against human judgments. While intuitively reasonable, there is no calibration showing that a 0.75-bit entropy drop corresponds to a perceptible increase in format homogeneity to human readers. The 50-character prefix threshold for near-duplicate detection is also arbitrary.\n\nW8. [MINOR] The quality metric uses the same ArmoRM model as the curation reward model, creating circular evaluation. The paper acknowledges reward saturation (score gap of ~0.04), but this circularity means \"quality stability\" is trivially expected when the model is trained to match the evaluator.\n\nW9. [MINOR] Table 1 is missing data (C6 nn-distance is \"---\", C7 quality is \"---\"). Incomplete tables in a submission suggest rushed execution.\n\n### Questions for Authors\nQ1. Have you attempted to run Qwen2.5-0.5B or Qwen3-1.7B under the same protocol to disentangle scale from architecture? Even one additional data point within the same model family would substantially strengthen the scale-dependent claim.\n\nQ2. What is the variance of Vendi Score across different random seeds for generation and training? If you ran C1 three times with different seeds, what range of Vendi deltas would you observe? Without this, the entire significance of <1% changes cannot be assessed.\n\nQ3. The curated 1.5B experiment completed but the random baseline OOM'd due to length distribution differences. This means the curated training data has systematically shorter sequences than a random sample. Could this implicit length filtering, rather than reward quality, be responsible for the different diversity dynamics? Have you measured the length distribution of curated vs. random selected data at 1.5B?\n\nQ4. For the direction reversal, C4 (Full-FT low-LR, curated) is labeled in Table 1 as \"Full-FT (lo)\" but no matched random baseline for this regime is presented in the paper's Tables. C6 in Table 1 is \"Full-FT (hi) random.\" Where is the Full-FT (lo) random configuration needed to compute the intermediate point of the three-regime gradient?\n\nQ5. Why was LoRA rank fixed at 16 for 0.6B and also used at 1.5B? At 1.5B, rank 16 represents a much smaller fraction of parameters (~0.5% vs ~1.67%), which means the capacity constraint is even tighter. Have you considered that the 1.5B collapse might be partly driven by LoRA rank being proportionally smaller?\n\n### Missing Experiments\nM1. [CRITICAL] Qwen2.5-1.5B LoRA K=4 random baseline (5 generations). This is the single most important missing experiment. Without it, the paper's headline finding is an uncontrolled observation. Even completing 3 of the 5 generations would provide substantial value.\n\nM2. [CRITICAL] Same-family scale comparison (either Qwen3 at a larger scale, or Qwen2.5 at a smaller scale) to separate the scale variable from the architecture variable. Without this, \"scale-dependent phase transition\" is an unsupported interpretation.\n\nM3. [MAJOR] Repeated runs (at least 3 seeds) for a subset of key configurations (e.g., C1, C5, C3, C6) to establish variance estimates and enable significance testing. Currently all claims rest on single-run point estimates.\n\nM4. [MAJOR] K > 4 at 1.5B scale (K=8 or K=16). The K x scale interaction is completely unexplored. If K=4 already produces collapse at 1.5B, K=16 could produce catastrophic collapse, which would be important to know.\n\nM5. [MINOR] Human evaluation of format diversity to validate the proposed format metrics. A small study asking annotators to judge whether response sets feel \"repetitive\" or \"diverse\" and correlating with the format metrics would substantially strengthen the measurement contribution.\n\n### Score: 3/6\n### Confidence: 4/5\n\nThe 0.6B factorial study is thorough and internally valid, and the format-vs-semantic metric separation is a useful methodological contribution. However, the paper's framing and title promise a scale-dependent finding that the evidence cannot support. The confound between model family and scale, the missing 1.5B control, the absence of any statistical testing, and the very real possibility that 0.6B is simply below the capacity threshold for the studied phenomenon collectively prevent acceptance. This work would benefit from completing the 1.5B random baseline, adding a same-family scale comparison, and reframing the contribution around what is actually well-supported: the 0.6B factorial analysis and the metric-dependent collapse observation."}],"totalDurationMs":131520,"totalTokens":95725,"totalToolUseCount":23,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9908,"cache_read_input_tokens":83314,"output_tokens":2502,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":9908},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":2502,"cache_read_input_tokens":83314,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9908,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":9908,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":19,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":2}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a2b9968546496cfca","tool_use_id":"toolu_013w4D8h5fQdQZT973z4cAnC","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":65780,"tool_uses":25,"duration_ms":125739},"uuid":"df521630-3f83-40d6-8014-e735237b1742","session_id":"2b1f599e-fc53-49d6-adbf-f2a150d3d4f3"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013w4D8h5fQdQZT973z4cAnC","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a thorough understanding of the paper. Let me also check the paper title mentioned in the review prompt versus the actual paper.\n\nThe review prompt describes the paper title as \"When Does Curation Become Dangerous? Scale-Dependent Diversity Collapse in Iterative Reward-Based Training\" with claims about a \"phase transition\" between 0.6B and 1.5B. However, the actual submitted paper (in `docs/paper/`) uses a different title (\"Does Reward Curation Kill Diversity?\") and does NOT include 1.5B results (the 1.5B experiments were killed per the report). The dream report version DOES include 1.5B data (C9, C10) but presents them as showing resilience, not collapse.\n\nLet me verify which version is the one being reviewed based on the review task description.\n\nThe review task says:\n- Title: \"When Does Curation Become Dangerous? Scale-Dependent Diversity Collapse in Iterative Reward-Based Training\"\n- Claims include: sub-1B resilience, scale-dependent phase transition (0.6B stable, 1.5B collapsing), causal decomposition of format collapse, capacity artifact\n- The 1.5B random baseline was killed (CUDA OOM)\n- 0.6B and 1.5B use different model families\n\nThis seems to be describing a version of the paper that goes beyond what's in the current `docs/paper/` directory. The dream report's \"best\" version claims all findings replicate at 1.5B (no phase transition), while the review context describes a paper claiming a phase transition. Given the review prompt's specific description, I should review the paper as described in the prompt context, which seems to synthesize across the available materials and the project decisions.\n\nLet me now write my review.\n\n---\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\nThis paper studies diversity dynamics under iterative reward-based curation in self-training loops, running 8 configurations on Qwen3-0.6B and a cross-scale probe on Qwen2.5-1.5B. The central claims are that semantic diversity is resilient at sub-1B scale, that a \"phase transition\" occurs between 0.6B and 1.5B, that format collapse is inherent to iterative training rather than curation-caused, and that LoRA creates capacity artifacts in diversity measurement. The paper cites only 8 references in the main submission (docs/paper version) or 11 in the dream report version, both substantially below typical venue expectations.\n\n### Strengths\n\nS1. **Well-designed factorial experiment.** The 8-configuration grid on 0.6B isolating adaptation method, K, learning rate, and curation strategy is methodologically sound. The C1-vs-C5 same-K controlled comparison for format collapse is the paper's strongest analytical contribution.\n\nS2. **Format-vs-semantic dissociation is a genuine contribution.** The observation that embedding-based metrics (Vendi Score) miss format-level homogenization while opening entropy and near-duplicate start rate capture it fills a real measurement gap. The proposed metrics are simple, computationally cheap, and practically useful.\n\nS3. **Direction reversal across capacity regimes is interesting.** The monotonic progression from LoRA suppression (-3.40 pp) through conservative Full-FT (-0.46 pp) to aggressive Full-FT promotion (+0.63 pp) is a clean empirical pattern. The capacity-mediated interpretation is plausible and has practical implications for how LoRA-based curation studies should be interpreted.\n\nS4. **Honest engagement with negative results.** The paper does not hide that the original CARR method failed or that the core \"collapse\" hypothesis was falsified. Pivoting to an empirical analysis paper rather than manufacturing a positive method result reflects scientific integrity.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\nW1. **[CRITICAL] The \"phase transition\" claim is unsupported.** The paper claims a qualitative shift between 0.6B (stable) and 1.5B (collapsing), but this is based on exactly two scale points using different model families (Qwen3 vs Qwen2.5), different architectures, different pretraining data, and different instruction-tuning methods. The 1.5B random baseline was killed due to CUDA OOM, so the curated-vs-random gap at 1.5B cannot be computed. Calling this a \"phase transition\" requires at minimum: (a) multiple scale points showing discontinuous behavior, (b) controlled comparison where architecture/family is held constant, and (c) a completed random baseline. None of these are satisfied. Two data points from confounded conditions do not establish a phase transition. The term should be removed entirely.\n\nW2. **[CRITICAL] Reference count is grossly insufficient.** The main submission cites only 8 substantive references (excluding toolkit/model citations). The dream report version cites 11. A top-venue empirical study in this area requires engagement with a much broader literature. For context, the competing papers identified in the project's own research notes (DIVE, DARLING, CD-RLHF, GEM, Kirk et al.) represent the minimum set that a Related Work section must engage with, and the current paper cites only a subset of even this small list in the docs/paper version. The reference density is approximately 3-4x below what reviewers expect at EMNLP/ACL.\n\nW3. **[MAJOR] Novelty is limited to empirical observations with no mechanistic explanation.** The paper provides no formal or semi-formal account of why semantic diversity is resilient. Section 5.2 lists three \"hypothesized contributing factors\" (prompt diversity, reward saturation, generation stochasticity) but does not test them individually through ablation. Without ablating temperature, prompt diversity, or reward model discriminativeness, the paper cannot distinguish between \"these factors each contribute\" and \"any one of them suffices.\" The observation is interesting; the understanding remains shallow.\n\nW4. **[MAJOR] Scale of experiments is too small for the generality of claims.** A single model at 596M parameters, 2,200 prompts, 3 generations, and a single reward model. The paper's practical recommendations (\"practitioners using reward-based curation in iterative SFT pipelines do not need to engineer semantic diversity safeguards\") dramatically overshoot what this evidence supports. Real iterative training pipelines operate at 7B-70B scale, 100K+ prompts, and 10+ iterations. The gap between the experimental setting and the claim domain is large enough to be disqualifying for the practical recommendation.\n\nW5. **[MAJOR] Format collapse contribution is weakened by metric simplicity and lack of validation.** Opening entropy over first-10-token sequences and near-duplicate 50-character prefix rate are ad hoc. There is no human evaluation confirming that these metrics correlate with perceived repetitiveness. There is no comparison with existing structural diversity metrics (e.g., parse tree diversity, discourse structure analysis). The metrics capture one specific failure mode (opening convergence) but the paper generalizes to \"format collapse\" without evidence that the body and conclusion of responses are not also homogenizing in ways these metrics miss (acknowledged in Limitations but still claimed broadly elsewhere).\n\nW6. **[MINOR] The direction reversal effect size is tiny.** The reversal from -3.40 pp (LoRA) to +0.63 pp (Full-FT 2e-5) is presented as a major finding, but the +0.63 pp promotion effect is within noise for a 200-sample evaluation set. No confidence intervals or significance tests are reported for any metric. The entire reversal narrative rests on the sign of a small number without statistical validation.\n\n### Missing Related Work\n\nMR1. **Best-of-N sampling / rejection sampling literature.** The paper's core mechanism (generate K, select top-1) is exactly best-of-N sampling. The extensive literature on this (e.g., Nakano et al. 2021 WebGPT; Stiennon et al. 2020 Learning to Summarize; Coste et al. 2023 Reward Model Ensembles; Beirami et al. 2024 theoretical analysis of best-of-N) is completely absent. This is a critical omission because these works analyze quality-diversity tradeoffs in the exact selection mechanism the paper studies.\n\nMR2. **Guo et al. (2024) \"Curious Decline of Linguistic Diversity.\"** This work specifically studies diversity decline in LLM outputs and is directly relevant. Not cited.\n\nMR3. **Briesch et al. (2023), Hataya et al. (2023).** Additional model collapse works that provide theoretical or empirical perspectives the paper should engage with for complete coverage.\n\nMR4. **SPIN (Chen et al. 2024) and Self-Play Fine-Tuning literature.** Iterative self-play methods that are structurally similar to the paper's iterative curation loop. SPIN specifically involves iterative generation and training cycles with selection. Not cited.\n\nMR5. **Data quality filtering literature (FineWeb, DSIR, Xie et al. 2023).** The paper studies quality-based filtering in iterative training. The data curation literature provides context for how filtering affects distributional properties and should be cited.\n\nMR6. **Perez et al. (2022) on RLHF and sycophancy/homogenization.** Early work documenting how RLHF reduces response diversity. Not cited.\n\nMR7. **DPO diversity literature (Rafailov et al. 2023, Azar et al. 2024).** Offline alignment methods that have known diversity implications and are part of the iterative alignment pipeline landscape.\n\nMR8. **Nucleus sampling / temperature diversity (Holtzman et al. 2020).** Cited in the dream report version but absent from the main submission. Since decoding temperature is listed as a key protective factor, the paper must cite the foundational work on stochastic decoding and diversity.\n\n### Framing Issues\n\nF1. **[CRITICAL] The title \"When Does Curation Become Dangerous?\" promises a threshold characterization that the paper cannot deliver.** The paper identifies one setting where curation is safe (0.6B) and claims (without complete evidence) that a different setting may not be safe (1.5B). This does not answer \"when\" in any generalizable way. A more honest title would reference the specific finding: resilience at small scale, or the format-vs-semantic dissociation.\n\nF2. **[MAJOR] Practical recommendations overshoot the evidence.** \"Practitioners using reward-based curation in iterative SFT pipelines do not need to engineer semantic diversity safeguards\" is stated as a general recommendation based on one sub-1B model with 2,200 prompts and 3 generations. This is an empirical observation about a narrow regime, not actionable guidance for production systems.\n\nF3. **[MAJOR] The paper simultaneously claims resilience is general (contribution 1) and scale-dependent (the phase transition claim).** These are in tension. If semantic diversity is resilient across \"all configurations\" at 0.6B but collapses at 1.5B, the primary contribution should be framed around the boundary conditions, not around resilience. The narrative wants to have it both ways.\n\nF4. **[MINOR] \"Metric-dependent collapse\" is misleading terminology.** What the paper describes is not \"collapse\" in the model collapse sense (progressive degeneration). It is format homogenization that occurs equally under curated and random conditions. Calling this \"metric-dependent collapse\" conflates two distinct phenomena and will confuse readers familiar with the model collapse literature. \"Format homogenization under iterative training\" would be more precise.\n\nF5. **[MINOR] The \"causal decomposition\" claim (contribution 2) is verified only at 0.6B with LoRA K=4.** The C1-vs-C5 comparison is the only matched curated/random pair used for the format collapse analysis. The paper does not verify this finding under Full-FT, other K values, or at 1.5B (since the random baseline was killed). Calling this a \"causal decomposition\" based on a single controlled comparison overstates the evidence.\n\n### Score: 3/6\n\n### Confidence: 4/5\n\nThe paper tackles a relevant question and contains some genuinely useful observations (format-vs-semantic dissociation, LoRA capacity artifact), but the evidence base is too narrow for the claims made, the \"phase transition\" framing is unjustified by the data, the related work coverage is critically insufficient, and the novelty is primarily observational without mechanistic depth. At a top venue with 25% acceptance rate, the combination of overclaiming, thin references, and limited experimental scale places this below the acceptance threshold."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a2b9968546496cfca (use SendMessage with to: 'a2b9968546496cfca' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 67908\ntool_uses: 25\nduration_ms: 125741</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2b1f599e-fc53-49d6-adbf-f2a150d3d4f3","uuid":"36456d33-bfdf-486e-bee6-000a7fbb004b","timestamp":"2026-05-17T14:45:04.413Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **novelty, positioning, and related work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"When Does Curation Become Dangerous? Scale-Dependent Diversity Collapse in Iterative Reward-Based Training\"\n\nThe paper studies diversity collapse in iterative self-consuming training with reward-based curation. It runs 8 configurations on Qwen3-0.6B (varying K∈{4,8,16}, LoRA vs full-FT, LR, curated vs random) over 3 generations, plus a cross-scale probe on Qwen2.5-1.5B over 5 generations.\n\nKey claims:\n1. Sub-1B resilience: reward-based curation does not cause semantic diversity collapse at 0.6B, even under extreme selection pressure.\n2. Scale-dependent phase transition: qualitative shift between 0.6B (stable) and 1.5B (collapsing) under identical protocols.\n3. Causal decomposition of format collapse: format homogenization is inherent to iterative self-training, not curation-caused (verified at 0.6B only).\n4. Capacity artifact: LoRA's low-rank constraint creates misleading diversity suppression, with direction reversal under full-FT across 3 capacity regimes.\n\n## Related Work Section (from paper)\nThe paper covers 4 areas:\n1. **Model collapse in self-consuming loops**: Shumailov et al. (2023), Alemohammad et al. (2023), Dohmatob et al. (2024). Paper claims to differ by studying reward-based curation specifically and finding scale-dependence.\n2. **Reward over-optimization**: Gao et al. (2023). Links 1.5B quality↑/diversity↓ pattern to reward over-optimization literature.\n3. **Diversity metrics**: Vendi Score (Friedman & Dieng 2023), Self-BLEU. Claims embedding metrics are blind to format homogenization.\n4. **LoRA and training capacity**: Hu et al. (2022). Claims capacity limitation creates measurement artifacts.\n\nTotal references: 8.\n\n## Key Context\n- The 1.5B random baseline was killed (CUDA OOM), so the \"phase transition\" claim cannot be causally verified.\n- 0.6B and 1.5B use different model families (Qwen3 vs Qwen2.5), confounding scale and architecture.\n- Only 2 scale points (0.6B, 1.5B) for the \"phase transition\" claim.\n- The paper pivoted from \"universal resilience\" narrative to \"scale-conditional analysis\" after 1.5B results broke the original framing.\n- All 0.6B experiments are on a single model family.\n\n## Your Review Task\nEvaluate the paper on:\n1. **Novelty**: Is the contribution sufficiently novel for a top venue? Is the \"phase transition\" framing justified by the evidence? Is the observation vs. mechanism distinction clear?\n2. **Related work coverage**: Are key related works cited? Is positioning against prior work accurate? Are there critical omissions?\n3. **Framing and claims**: Are claims appropriately stated? Is there overclaiming? Is the narrative coherent?\n4. **Significance and impact**: Would this paper change how practitioners or researchers think about iterative curation?\n\nFor each issue, classify severity as CRITICAL / MAJOR / MINOR.\n\nImportant related work areas to check coverage of:\n- Model collapse literature (Shumailov, Alemohammad, Dohmatob, Guo et al., Briesch et al., Hataya et al.)\n- RLHF/alignment diversity concerns (Kirk et al., Perez et al.)\n- Best-of-N sampling and rejection sampling literature\n- Data curation and quality filtering (e.g., FineWeb, DSIR)\n- Iterative DPO/self-play methods (SPIN, Self-Play Fine-Tuning)\n- Diversity-preserving training methods\n\nOutput your review in this format:\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\nS1. ...\nS2. ...\n\n### Weaknesses\nW1. [SEVERITY] ...\nW2. [SEVERITY] ...\n\n### Missing Related Work\nMR1. ...\n\n### Framing Issues\nF1. ...\n\n### Score: X/6\n### Confidence: X/5\n```\n","agentId":"a2b9968546496cfca","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a thorough understanding of the paper. Let me also check the paper title mentioned in the review prompt versus the actual paper.\n\nThe review prompt describes the paper title as \"When Does Curation Become Dangerous? Scale-Dependent Diversity Collapse in Iterative Reward-Based Training\" with claims about a \"phase transition\" between 0.6B and 1.5B. However, the actual submitted paper (in `docs/paper/`) uses a different title (\"Does Reward Curation Kill Diversity?\") and does NOT include 1.5B results (the 1.5B experiments were killed per the report). The dream report version DOES include 1.5B data (C9, C10) but presents them as showing resilience, not collapse.\n\nLet me verify which version is the one being reviewed based on the review task description.\n\nThe review task says:\n- Title: \"When Does Curation Become Dangerous? Scale-Dependent Diversity Collapse in Iterative Reward-Based Training\"\n- Claims include: sub-1B resilience, scale-dependent phase transition (0.6B stable, 1.5B collapsing), causal decomposition of format collapse, capacity artifact\n- The 1.5B random baseline was killed (CUDA OOM)\n- 0.6B and 1.5B use different model families\n\nThis seems to be describing a version of the paper that goes beyond what's in the current `docs/paper/` directory. The dream report's \"best\" version claims all findings replicate at 1.5B (no phase transition), while the review context describes a paper claiming a phase transition. Given the review prompt's specific description, I should review the paper as described in the prompt context, which seems to synthesize across the available materials and the project decisions.\n\nLet me now write my review.\n\n---\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\nThis paper studies diversity dynamics under iterative reward-based curation in self-training loops, running 8 configurations on Qwen3-0.6B and a cross-scale probe on Qwen2.5-1.5B. The central claims are that semantic diversity is resilient at sub-1B scale, that a \"phase transition\" occurs between 0.6B and 1.5B, that format collapse is inherent to iterative training rather than curation-caused, and that LoRA creates capacity artifacts in diversity measurement. The paper cites only 8 references in the main submission (docs/paper version) or 11 in the dream report version, both substantially below typical venue expectations.\n\n### Strengths\n\nS1. **Well-designed factorial experiment.** The 8-configuration grid on 0.6B isolating adaptation method, K, learning rate, and curation strategy is methodologically sound. The C1-vs-C5 same-K controlled comparison for format collapse is the paper's strongest analytical contribution.\n\nS2. **Format-vs-semantic dissociation is a genuine contribution.** The observation that embedding-based metrics (Vendi Score) miss format-level homogenization while opening entropy and near-duplicate start rate capture it fills a real measurement gap. The proposed metrics are simple, computationally cheap, and practically useful.\n\nS3. **Direction reversal across capacity regimes is interesting.** The monotonic progression from LoRA suppression (-3.40 pp) through conservative Full-FT (-0.46 pp) to aggressive Full-FT promotion (+0.63 pp) is a clean empirical pattern. The capacity-mediated interpretation is plausible and has practical implications for how LoRA-based curation studies should be interpreted.\n\nS4. **Honest engagement with negative results.** The paper does not hide that the original CARR method failed or that the core \"collapse\" hypothesis was falsified. Pivoting to an empirical analysis paper rather than manufacturing a positive method result reflects scientific integrity.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\nW1. **[CRITICAL] The \"phase transition\" claim is unsupported.** The paper claims a qualitative shift between 0.6B (stable) and 1.5B (collapsing), but this is based on exactly two scale points using different model families (Qwen3 vs Qwen2.5), different architectures, different pretraining data, and different instruction-tuning methods. The 1.5B random baseline was killed due to CUDA OOM, so the curated-vs-random gap at 1.5B cannot be computed. Calling this a \"phase transition\" requires at minimum: (a) multiple scale points showing discontinuous behavior, (b) controlled comparison where architecture/family is held constant, and (c) a completed random baseline. None of these are satisfied. Two data points from confounded conditions do not establish a phase transition. The term should be removed entirely.\n\nW2. **[CRITICAL] Reference count is grossly insufficient.** The main submission cites only 8 substantive references (excluding toolkit/model citations). The dream report version cites 11. A top-venue empirical study in this area requires engagement with a much broader literature. For context, the competing papers identified in the project's own research notes (DIVE, DARLING, CD-RLHF, GEM, Kirk et al.) represent the minimum set that a Related Work section must engage with, and the current paper cites only a subset of even this small list in the docs/paper version. The reference density is approximately 3-4x below what reviewers expect at EMNLP/ACL.\n\nW3. **[MAJOR] Novelty is limited to empirical observations with no mechanistic explanation.** The paper provides no formal or semi-formal account of why semantic diversity is resilient. Section 5.2 lists three \"hypothesized contributing factors\" (prompt diversity, reward saturation, generation stochasticity) but does not test them individually through ablation. Without ablating temperature, prompt diversity, or reward model discriminativeness, the paper cannot distinguish between \"these factors each contribute\" and \"any one of them suffices.\" The observation is interesting; the understanding remains shallow.\n\nW4. **[MAJOR] Scale of experiments is too small for the generality of claims.** A single model at 596M parameters, 2,200 prompts, 3 generations, and a single reward model. The paper's practical recommendations (\"practitioners using reward-based curation in iterative SFT pipelines do not need to engineer semantic diversity safeguards\") dramatically overshoot what this evidence supports. Real iterative training pipelines operate at 7B-70B scale, 100K+ prompts, and 10+ iterations. The gap between the experimental setting and the claim domain is large enough to be disqualifying for the practical recommendation.\n\nW5. **[MAJOR] Format collapse contribution is weakened by metric simplicity and lack of validation.** Opening entropy over first-10-token sequences and near-duplicate 50-character prefix rate are ad hoc. There is no human evaluation confirming that these metrics correlate with perceived repetitiveness. There is no comparison with existing structural diversity metrics (e.g., parse tree diversity, discourse structure analysis). The metrics capture one specific failure mode (opening convergence) but the paper generalizes to \"format collapse\" without evidence that the body and conclusion of responses are not also homogenizing in ways these metrics miss (acknowledged in Limitations but still claimed broadly elsewhere).\n\nW6. **[MINOR] The direction reversal effect size is tiny.** The reversal from -3.40 pp (LoRA) to +0.63 pp (Full-FT 2e-5) is presented as a major finding, but the +0.63 pp promotion effect is within noise for a 200-sample evaluation set. No confidence intervals or significance tests are reported for any metric. The entire reversal narrative rests on the sign of a small number without statistical validation.\n\n### Missing Related Work\n\nMR1. **Best-of-N sampling / rejection sampling literature.** The paper's core mechanism (generate K, select top-1) is exactly best-of-N sampling. The extensive literature on this (e.g., Nakano et al. 2021 WebGPT; Stiennon et al. 2020 Learning to Summarize; Coste et al. 2023 Reward Model Ensembles; Beirami et al. 2024 theoretical analysis of best-of-N) is completely absent. This is a critical omission because these works analyze quality-diversity tradeoffs in the exact selection mechanism the paper studies.\n\nMR2. **Guo et al. (2024) \"Curious Decline of Linguistic Diversity.\"** This work specifically studies diversity decline in LLM outputs and is directly relevant. Not cited.\n\nMR3. **Briesch et al. (2023), Hataya et al. (2023).** Additional model collapse works that provide theoretical or empirical perspectives the paper should engage with for complete coverage.\n\nMR4. **SPIN (Chen et al. 2024) and Self-Play Fine-Tuning literature.** Iterative self-play methods that are structurally similar to the paper's iterative curation loop. SPIN specifically involves iterative generation and training cycles with selection. Not cited.\n\nMR5. **Data quality filtering literature (FineWeb, DSIR, Xie et al. 2023).** The paper studies quality-based filtering in iterative training. The data curation literature provides context for how filtering affects distributional properties and should be cited.\n\nMR6. **Perez et al. (2022) on RLHF and sycophancy/homogenization.** Early work documenting how RLHF reduces response diversity. Not cited.\n\nMR7. **DPO diversity literature (Rafailov et al. 2023, Azar et al. 2024).** Offline alignment methods that have known diversity implications and are part of the iterative alignment pipeline landscape.\n\nMR8. **Nucleus sampling / temperature diversity (Holtzman et al. 2020).** Cited in the dream report version but absent from the main submission. Since decoding temperature is listed as a key protective factor, the paper must cite the foundational work on stochastic decoding and diversity.\n\n### Framing Issues\n\nF1. **[CRITICAL] The title \"When Does Curation Become Dangerous?\" promises a threshold characterization that the paper cannot deliver.** The paper identifies one setting where curation is safe (0.6B) and claims (without complete evidence) that a different setting may not be safe (1.5B). This does not answer \"when\" in any generalizable way. A more honest title would reference the specific finding: resilience at small scale, or the format-vs-semantic dissociation.\n\nF2. **[MAJOR] Practical recommendations overshoot the evidence.** \"Practitioners using reward-based curation in iterative SFT pipelines do not need to engineer semantic diversity safeguards\" is stated as a general recommendation based on one sub-1B model with 2,200 prompts and 3 generations. This is an empirical observation about a narrow regime, not actionable guidance for production systems.\n\nF3. **[MAJOR] The paper simultaneously claims resilience is general (contribution 1) and scale-dependent (the phase transition claim).** These are in tension. If semantic diversity is resilient across \"all configurations\" at 0.6B but collapses at 1.5B, the primary contribution should be framed around the boundary conditions, not around resilience. The narrative wants to have it both ways.\n\nF4. **[MINOR] \"Metric-dependent collapse\" is misleading terminology.** What the paper describes is not \"collapse\" in the model collapse sense (progressive degeneration). It is format homogenization that occurs equally under curated and random conditions. Calling this \"metric-dependent collapse\" conflates two distinct phenomena and will confuse readers familiar with the model collapse literature. \"Format homogenization under iterative training\" would be more precise.\n\nF5. **[MINOR] The \"causal decomposition\" claim (contribution 2) is verified only at 0.6B with LoRA K=4.** The C1-vs-C5 comparison is the only matched curated/random pair used for the format collapse analysis. The paper does not verify this finding under Full-FT, other K values, or at 1.5B (since the random baseline was killed). Calling this a \"causal decomposition\" based on a single controlled comparison overstates the evidence.\n\n### Score: 3/6\n\n### Confidence: 4/5\n\nThe paper tackles a relevant question and contains some genuinely useful observations (format-vs-semantic dissociation, LoRA capacity artifact), but the evidence base is too narrow for the claims made, the \"phase transition\" framing is unjustified by the data, the related work coverage is critically insufficient, and the novelty is primarily observational without mechanistic depth. At a top venue with 25% acceptance rate, the combination of overclaiming, thin references, and limited experimental scale places this below the acceptance threshold."}],"totalDurationMs":125741,"totalTokens":67908,"totalToolUseCount":25,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6607,"cache_read_input_tokens":58326,"output_tokens":2974,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6607},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":2974,"cache_read_input_tokens":58326,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6